d\\n?? r3-fix-wt/\\n?? r3_apply.py\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-9EncC9Ty' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 23:58:11.752 xcodebuild[20244:123135031]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 23:58:12.046 xcodebuild[20244:123135030] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-cGHLIPf8' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-11 23:58:13.047 xcodebuild[20257:123135121]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-11 23:58:13.347 xcodebuild[20257:123135119] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\nfatal: ambiguous argument 'adb6af80..ff8b980a': unknown revision or path not in the working tree.\\nUse '--' to separate paths from revisions, like this:\\n'git <command> [<revision>...] -- [<file>...]'\\n\",\"exit_code\":128,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md; cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: code-review-pr\\ndescription: >-\\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\\n---\\n\\n# Code Review PR Skill\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\\n\\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\\n\\n```bash\\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\\n\\n# 2. Review last commit\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\\n\\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\\n\\n# 4. Save review report to file\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Code Review Workflow\\n\\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\\n\\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\\n\\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\\n\\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\\n\\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\\n\\n```markdown\\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\\n\\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\\n  ```diff\\n  - problematic code\\n  + suggested fix\\n  ```\\n\\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\\n\\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\\n\\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Utility Script Reference\\n\\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\\n\\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Common Review Pitfalls\\n\\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\\n---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/exception-track/1794451000000-consult-vm-provenance-design.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; cat collab/exception-track/1794452000000-engineer-vm-provenance-primitive.findings.md; command -v -a git; ls /opt/homebrew/bin/git /usr/local/bin/git; cat .git'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/exception-track/1794451000000-consult-vm-provenance-design.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; cat collab/exception-track/1794452000000-engineer-vm-provenance-primitive.findings.md; command -v -a git; ls /opt/homebrew/bin/git /usr/local/bin/git; cat .git'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-11 16:25:13 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 23:25:13 +07 (+0700)\\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol)\\nRole: Architect (owner condition (a) design consult + condition (b) cross-frontend ruling)\\nStatus: CONSULT VERDICT OF RECORD — APPROVED WITH AMENDMENTS\\n\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-11 16:25:13 GMT  \\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 23:25:13 +07 (+0700)\\n\\nThe heap-entry design is sound **with amendments for GC tracing, mint authority through macros and runtime helpers, and Clojure adoption**. This ruling concerns retention under the continuation encoding. The owner’s convergence directive remains binding. ([FINAL DIRECTIVE](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794372000000-owner-decision-r3-vm-retained-seam.md:90))\\n\\nRead-only inspection covered master `adb6af80`, Python’s committed contract `0326a044`, and removal commit `e735ebb2` and its parent. No tests were run or files changed.\\n\\n1. **Heap-entry storage: approved.**\\n\\n   Store the complete retained condition in an optional, private sibling of `:value` and `:seal`. I recommend the kernel-neutral name **`:provenance`**:\\n\\n   ```clojure\\n   {:seal seal\\n    :value guest-visible-content\\n    :provenance original-condition}\\n   ```\\n\\n   The VM treats provenance as opaque data. It knows no frontend, exception class, qualified reason, or condition schema.\\n\\n   Existing cell access supports this boundary: `cell/get` returns only `:value`; `cell/set!` supplies a replacement value to `heap-write`. Authentication uses the live heap entry and its seal. ([access](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2773), [authentication](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:332))\\n\\n   The historical preservation design was appropriate: `engine.cljc:2457–2472 @ e735ebb2^` updated existing entry fields without discarding sibling facts and allocated the marked entry separately. Its limitation for this proposal is that it retained only a boolean mark; the condition still lived in mutable `:value`.\\n\\n   A separate id map duplicates lifetime bookkeeping. A closure carrying condition data does not, by itself, bind that data to the wrapper’s heap identity. The entry already supplies the required identity and lifetime.\\n\\n2. **Write-once enforcement: preserve provenance; do not refuse `cell/set!`.**\\n\\n   Current `heap-write` replaces the entire entry with `{:value v :seal seal}` and therefore **must change**. It must preserve existing sibling fields while updating the value. ([engine.cljc:2444](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2444))\\n\\n   Required semantics:\\n\\n   - Only the privileged mint creates `:provenance`, while allocating a fresh id.\\n   - Ordinary `cell/new` creates an unmarked entry.\\n   - No operation attaches, replaces, or clears provenance on an existing entry.\\n   - `cell/set!` preserves provenance and the existing seal.\\n   - The reader never obtains retention from `:value`.\\n\\n   **No explicit marked-cell write refusal is needed.** The old refusal protected condition data stored inside `:value`; moving the complete condition outside that value removes that requirement. Allowing visible mutations also preserves ordinary mutable exception attributes.\\n\\n   Consequently, copying a wrapper’s contents into a new cell loses provenance; overwriting the original wrapper’s visible contents cannot replace its retained condition. Copying its sealed reference still names the same object and legitimately retains rethrow authority.\\n\\n3. **Doors: one shared reserved pair, with a mandatory macro-admission amendment.**\\n\\n   Adopt these exact VM bindings:\\n\\n   | Binding | Arity | Semantics |\\n   |---|---:|---|\\n   | `cell/dsg__new-provenance` | 2: `[value provenance]` | Allocate a fresh sealed cell with both fields installed atomically. Provenance must be non-nil and otherwise remains opaque. |\\n   | `cell/dsg__provenance` | 1: `[candidate]` | Return the stored provenance for an authenticated, live, marked cell; return nil otherwise. No mutation or failure for an unauthenticated candidate. |\\n\\n   Corresponding effects: `:cell/new-provenance` and `:cell/provenance`, with explicit module profiles declaring arities `[2]` and `[1]`. Existing cell bindings already use this effect-constructor/profile arrangement. ([module.cljc:388](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:388))\\n\\n   The query must authenticate **before** consulting the slot. Its authority is the task’s issuer-maintained heap entry, never a caller-supplied issuer label or condition-map shape. It must not require the current visible content to retain a particular frontend type tag.\\n\\n   The frontend findings are:\\n\\n   - **Clojure, raw source:** the pre-pass checks the symbol’s local name, including qualified heads, so both proposed names are rejected. Quoted symbols remain inert data. ([prefix check](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:418), [program check](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2343))\\n   - **Python:** source names resolve through Python lexical/module lookup; qualified cell operations come from compiler-generated calls. ([lower.cljc:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:55), [global lookup](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:452))\\n   - **PHP:** source variables and function calls use PHP scope/name resolution and call helpers, without arbitrary `cell/NAME` pass-through. ([variables](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:635), [calls](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1639))\\n   - **JS/TypeScript:** source identifiers resolve through lexical cells or JS globals. A spelling containing `/` does not become a qualified VM variable. ([lower.cljc:537](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:537))\\n\\n   **The existing regime does not establish protection through macro expansion.** The macro prelude can construct an arbitrary variable from a symbol, while VM Rule R currently reserves only `yin/def`. ([macro.cljc:948](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:948), [vm.cljc:113](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:113)) The earlier review expressly left this as S2; its ordinary `compile-program` probe did not exercise expansion. ([S2 evidence](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794371000000-reviewer-r3-A-design-confirm.glm-5.3.findings.md:128))\\n\\n   **Landing requirement:** source/macro admission must reject guest-created executable references, bindings, and definition targets for these doors. Preserve legitimate compiler-generated occurrences through trusted construction provenance; quoted names alone must confer no authority. Exercise the actual macro/REPL expansion path, including returning a door as a value for indirect application. A raw-source spelling test is insufficient.\\n\\n   Also protect the **transitive mint callers**. Python currently defines `py/condition-exception`, and its module exports every `py/` definition. Simply changing that helper to invoke the privileged mint would leave a constructor accepting arbitrary condition data on an exported surface. ([helper](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1043), [exports](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3753)) Provenance-writing helpers and private readers must use protected reserved names and stay outside guest exports, projections, and aliases.\\n\\n4. **GC-with-entry: approved, but sweep preservation alone is insufficient.**\\n\\n   Sweep removes whole entries, so the slot has no separate lifetime. However, normal marking currently follows only the referenced entry’s `:value`; pinned entries likewise seed tracing from only their values. Both must change. ([trace](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2577), [pinned roots](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2615), [sweep](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2627))\\n\\n   Required changes:\\n\\n   - Trace `:value` **and `:provenance`** as data from every live entry.\\n   - Do the same for pinned entries, which enter the found set before ordinary following.\\n   - Before mint-triggered collection, hold both incoming value and provenance as extra roots. Current allocation rooting covers only the boxed value. ([count-allocation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:2659))\\n   - Do not make provenance-bearing entries global roots or pin them merely because they carry provenance.\\n\\n   This preserves payload cells referenced only by the retained condition—even after a guest replaces the entire visible value. When the wrapper becomes unreachable, its condition and exclusively reachable payload cells become collectible. Existing exported-reference pinning still extends lifetime under the established task policy.\\n\\n   No evaluator lane reconstructs individual heap entries:\\n\\n   | Lane | Evidence |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | Semantic | Uses the shared engine and continues with its returned state. ([semantic.cljc:92](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:92)) |\\n   | de Bruijn stack | Carries the whole heap and uses shared effect dispatch. ([constructor](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:428), [dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc:610)) |\\n   | de Bruijn register | Carries the whole heap and uses shared effect dispatch. ([constructor](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:385), [dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:548)) |\\n   | AST walker | Threads the whole heap through its positional record and dispatches through the engine. ([heap](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:144), [dispatch](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:179)) |\\n\\n   Thus the storage change belongs in shared engine/module code, without new lane-specific heap representations or instructions. Update the documented entry shape. ([state.md:119](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md:119))\\n\\n5. **Cross-frontend adoption ruling.**\\n\\n   | Frontend | Ruling |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | **Python** | **Adopt now.** Mint generated ForeignException/protocol wrappers through the shared mint; replace visible-map retention reads with the authenticated query. Remove the visible stamp as an authority source. |\\n   | **PHP** | **Adopt at its round’s start.** Store provenance on the cell defining the generated Throwable object’s identity, rather than a transient holder or copied facade. Generated retained exceptions must use that cell-backed representation; the existing map-only error fallback cannot authenticate retention. ([current fallback](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:299)) |\\n   | **Clojure** | **The continuation port adopts this primitive for protocol-condition retention.** Replace the witness mechanism and retire its replay/mutation limitation once acceptance proves closure. |\\n   | **JS** | **Grandfather the shipped `js.rt` identity store.** Migration is optional and non-urgent; it must not block the three required consumers. |\\n\\n   **Clojure’s “no retention object needed” claim is incorrect.** The port brief makes that claim at [lines 45–48](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794400000000-engineer-port-clojure-continuations.prompt.md:45), but the contract explicitly requires recovery after the creating catch/call has ended. Temporary activation state is insufficient. ([architecture:3885](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3885))\\n\\n   A continuation preserves a qualified condition during transfer. Once source catches a local exception, stores it, and later executes `(throw saved)`, the new activation needs an identity-owned association to recover that condition. Without one, forwarding requires recognizing map contents, violating R5, or wrapping the value in a fresh `:yang.call/thrown`, violating R3.\\n\\n   Therefore retain an identity-bearing, documented local protocol exception form. Put its complete original condition in the private slot; expose message/evidence through local accessors. Ordinary arbitrary thrown payloads remain raw. A source-created condition-shaped map receives no provenance and remains a fresh thrown payload. The committed contract already distinguishes saved protocol exceptions from shape-return/shape-throw cases. ([fixtures](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj:126))\\n\\n   JS’s store authenticates membership using sealed wrapper identity and does not consult mutable wrapper metadata. Its documented tradeoff is retaining associations until task teardown. ([identity policy](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1652), [implementation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:4133)) A later migration would improve reclamation and remove linear lookup, without urgency as a forgery repair.\\n\\n6. **Uniformity: one primitive, one field, one door pair.**\\n\\n   **Do not introduce frontend-specific heap fields or VM mint/query variants.** Python, PHP, and Clojure use the same opaque `:provenance` slot and the same two bindings. Their private constructors choose the local exception representation; their boundary readers recover the complete condition through the shared authenticated query.\\n\\n   The engine performs no reason dispatch, frontend dispatch, class matching, rendering, or foreign method calls. Receiving runtimes interpret authenticated incoming conditions; escaping runtimes forward authenticated retained conditions unchanged. An unmarked thrown value always creates a fresh condition.\\n\\nThe unchanged `0326a044` forgery pins remain binding: forge and clone produce fresh thrown conditions carrying the clone; overwrite forwards the original condition. ([pins](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/python_exception_test.clj:111)) Add focused verification for provenance-only payload reachability, pinned wrappers, allocation-triggered GC, eventual reclamation, macro-door access, and exported-helper bypasses. These are implementation acceptance obligations, not results established by this read-only consult.\\n\\n**APPROVED WITH AMENDMENTS: (1) use one opaque `:provenance` field and shared reserved mint/read pair; (2) preserve it across writes without refusing `cell/set!`; (3) trace it through ordinary, pinned, and allocation-time GC roots; (4) close macro and transitive-helper mint access before landing; (5) amend the Clojure port to retain protocol exceptions through this primitive.**Created-GMT: 2026-11-11 16:30:00 GMT\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-11 16:52:32 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 23:52:33 +0700 (+0700)\\nRole: Engineer (VM round; branch owner: the continuation-semantics orchestrator)\\nCoding-Agent: deepseek\\n\\n# Findings — the VM provenance primitive (sol consult APPROVED WITH AMENDMENTS)\\n\\nLanded the sol-approved design on branch `exception-vm-provenance` (cut from\\norigin/master @ adb6af80), scoped strictly to the VM surface.\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- `yin.vm/module.cljc`: the `cell` module gains the two compiler-private doors\\n  `cell/dsg__new-provenance` (effect `:cell/new-provenance`, arity [2]) and\\n  `cell/dsg__provenance` (effect `:cell/provenance`, arity [1]), each an\\n  `:effectful` profile per module.cljc:388.\\n- `yin.vm/engine.cljc`: `heap-write` preserves `:provenance` while updating\\n  `:value`/`:seal`; the `:cell/new-provenance` arm allocates a fresh sealed\\n  entry with `:value`+`:seal`+`:provenance` atomically; the `:cell/provenance`\\n  arm authenticates before consulting the slot and returns nil otherwise\\n  (never fatal, never mutating). GC traces `:provenance` as data from live\\n  entries, from pinned entries, and as an extra root at allocation-triggered\\n  collection (`count-allocation` now takes `& roots`).\\n- `yin.vm.cljc`: `reserved-prefix` (\\\"dsg__\\\") and `reserved-prefix-name?`.\\n- `yin.vm/macro.cljc`: the expander prelude refuses a compiler-private name in\\n  `yin/variable`, `yin/lambda` (and `yin/make-lambda` via `lambda-packet`), and\\n  `yin/make-def` — a guest macro can no longer construct an executable\\n  reference, binder, or definition target for a door.\\n- `yin.vm/docs/state.md`: heap-entry shape (:119) now documents `:provenance`.\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n1. Unit tests (provenance_test.cljc): mint/read, write-once, query-nil-for-\\n   unmarked, set!-preserves-provenance, content-copy-loses-provenance,\\n   ref-copy-retains-rethrow-authority — all green on the four VMs.\\n2. GC probes: provenance-only payload reachability across ordinary, pinned,\\n   and allocation-triggered collection, plus eventual reclamation when the\\n   wrapper drops — green.\\n3. Macro-door access + exported-helper bypass tests — green (the full macro\\n   invocation path, including a door returned as a variable for indirect\\n   application, is refused).\\n4. VM seam suites green: cell, heap-reclamation, module, effect-forgery,\\n   rule-r, parity, mint, de Bruijn stack + register, semantic.\\n5. `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the changed files: 0 errors. One pre-existing\\n   \\\"Redundant let expression\\\" warning remains in `yin.vm.macro/construct`\\n   (untouched by this round).\\n6. `yang.call.exception-contract-test` was NOT run locally (owner ruling).\\n\\nSHA ff8b980a\\n\\n**PROVENANCE PRIMITIVE LANDED**\\nzsh:1: command not found: -v\\nls: /opt/homebrew/bin/git: No such file or directory\\nls: /usr/local/bin/git: No such file or directory\\ncat: .git: Is a directory\\n\",\"exit_code\":1,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'ls .git/refs/heads .git/refs/remotes/origin; cat .git/config; ls /Users/sto/workspace; ls /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/git; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'ls .git/refs/heads .git/refs/remotes/origin; cat .git/config; ls /Users/sto/workspace; ls /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/git; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\".git/refs/heads:\\ncc3-lowering-provenance\\nexception-js-r1r3\\nexception-php-r1r2r3\\nexception-py-r2r3\\nexception-r3-impl\\njolt-orch2\\nmaster\\nyin-repl-dht-composition\\n\\n.git/refs/remotes/origin:\\nHEAD\\ncc3-lowering-provenance\\ncodex\\nexception-js-r1r3\\nexception-py-r2r3\\nexception-r3-impl\\nmaster\\n[core]\\n\\trepositoryformatversion = 0\\n\\tfilemode = true\\n\\tbare = false\\n\\tlogallrefupdates = true\\n\\tignorecase = true\\n\\tprecomposeunicode = true\\n[remote \\\"origin\\\"]\\n\\turl = git@github.com:sonwh98/datomworld.git\\n\\tfetch = +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\n[branch \\\"master\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n\\tvscode-merge-base = origin/master\\n\\tpushRemote = origin\\n[pull]\\n\\trebase = false\\n[user]\\n\\temail = son.c.to@gmail.com\\n\\tname = Sonny To\\n[branch \\\"agent.tzu.postgraphics\\\"]\\n\\tpushRemote = origin\\n[branch \\\"dao.stream.whatsapp\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/dao.stream.whatsapp\\n\\tpushRemote = origin\\n[status]\\n\\tshowUntrackedFiles = all\\n[branch \\\"c4-i5\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/c4-i5\\n[branch \\\"c4-i6-r4\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/c4-i6-r4\\n[branch \\\"jolt-j5\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/jolt-j5\\n[branch \\\"wp-w0a\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"jolt-j4d-fixes\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/jolt-j4d-fixes\\n[extensions]\\n\\tworktreeConfig = true\\n[branch \\\"jolt-j6f\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"jolt-orch2\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"feat/p8-legacy-retirement\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"jolt-j6g\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/jolt-j6g\\n[branch \\\"feat/p5-oracle-recording\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"feat/p1-forward-fixes\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"feat/roundB1-p9-contracts\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"j6g-mob-astra\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"j6g-mob-qwen\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"j6g-mob-ds\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"feat/p5b2-lifecycle\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/feat/p5b2-lifecycle\\n[branch \\\"callsite-contract-clojure\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/callsite-contract-clojure\\n[branch \\\"cc2-transform-session-c1\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"roundC-p9-fixtures\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/roundC-p9-fixtures\\n[branch \\\"feat/p5b2-oracle-promotion\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/feat/p5b2-oracle-promotion\\n[branch \\\"exception-r3-impl\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/exception-r3-impl\\n[branch \\\"exception-py-r2r3\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/exception-py-r2r3\\n[branch \\\"cc3-lowering-provenance\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\n[branch \\\"exception-php-r1r2r3\\\"]\\n\\tremote = origin\\n\\tmerge = refs/heads/master\\nClojureDart\\ndatomworld\\ndatomworld-b1\\ndatomworld-b2\\ndatomworld-ble2\\ndatomworld-c12gate\\ndatomworld-c3key1\\ndatomworld-cc1\\ndatomworld-cc2\\ndatomworld-cc3\\ndatomworld-f12\\ndatomworld-j6f\\ndatomworld-j6g\\ndatomworld-j6g-mob-astra\\ndatomworld-j6g-mob-ds\\ndatomworld-j6g-mob-qwen\\ndatomworld-l1-l4\\ndatomworld-orch\\ndatomworld-p5rec\\ndatomworld-p8\\ndatomworld-phpexc\\ndatomworld-py-c2gen3.prerebase.patch\\ndatomworld-py-c2gen3.prerebase2.patch\\ndatomworld-py-c2gen3.prerebase3.patch\\ndatomworld-py-c3key1.prerebase.patch\\ndatomworld-py-c3key1.prerebase2.patch\\ndatomworld-py-safepoint2.prerebase.patch\\ndatomworld-py-safepoint2.prerebase2.patch\\ndatomworld-py-safepoint2.prewrap.patch\\ndatomworld-py-safepoint2.safepoint_programs.cljc.bak\\ndatomworld-py-safepoint2.safepoint_programs.cljc.bak2\\ndatomworld-r3\\ndatomworld-r3-orch\\ndatomworld-roundC\\ndatomworld-vmprov\\ndatomworld-wp-w0a\\ndatomworld-xlink\\ndatomworld-yang-s9\\ndatomworld-yin-repl-dht\\ndatomworld.bak\\nhead-test.log\\njolt\\nlambdacart\\nmosh-log.txt\\n/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/git\\n/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git\\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\n\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\n\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\n\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\n\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\n\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\n\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\n\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\n\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\n2. **Explain the \\\"Why\\\"**: Don't just say \\\"change this\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\").\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\n```\\n\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\n\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\n\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\n\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\n\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\n\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\n\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\\n\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\n\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\n\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\n\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\n\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>[/<test-name>]` (owner ruling\\n   2026-11-07: the focus rule runs the failing TEST, not the namespace —\\n   `NS/test-name` entries become flutter `--plain-name` filters so only the\\n   named deftests execute; fall back to bare `<ns>` only when the failing\\n   var inside the namespace is not yet known, and narrow as soon as it is).\\n   Fix against the failing test alone, re-run it green, then climb back up\\n   (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\\n   Dart bug-chases may set `DATOM_FAIL_FAST=1` (owner ruling 2026-11-07): the\\n   scoped run stops at the first failure so the report arrives in minutes instead\\n   of after the full scoped set. Gate runs never set it — the merge bar needs the\\n   complete pass/fail picture. All dart runs use `--reporter expanded`, which\\n   streams failure names and positions inline so an interrupted run still retains\\n   them (the nightly's compact reporter lost 7 failure names to its 30000s wall,\\n   2026-11-06).\\n   Merge bar for a fix branch (owner ruling 2026-11-07, sharpening the 2026-11-01\\n   ruling): when the work is fixing already-failing tests, the previously-failing\\n   tests re-run green IS the merge bar — a fix branch merges on that alone; the\\n   G1/G2 climb applies to new feature slices, not to fixes. The full suite still\\n   gets its end-to-end confirmation from the daily nightly (04:00 VN), which is\\n   what catches collateral damage, not a pre-merge suite run.\\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \\\"Cadence\\\" below), so a\\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). mise was moved off the apt package to the official user-local install (`~/.local/bin/mise`, 2026.11.2, owner action 2026-11-07) so both hosts share shiva's layout; toolchains in `~/.local/share/mise/installs/` were untouched. Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\\n\\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\\n\\\"nightly.clj\\\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\\nmistake, not a host outage.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\\n\\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\\ntree that did not contain them.\\n\\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\\ngit fetch -q origin \\\"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\\"\\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\\n```\\n\\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\"`). If\\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\\n\\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\\n\\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\\nfirst lane:\\n\\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\\n   only (\\\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\\\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\\n\\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\\n\\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\\n```\\n\\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\\nresource fails there immediately.\\n\\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\\n\\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\\nchange to a ledger file:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\\n```\\n\\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\\nstaging.\\n\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\n\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\n5-6-hour run.\\n\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\n\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\n\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \\\"a second UDP bind on\\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\\\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\n\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\nchanges\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\"it should not be for\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\"\\n\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\n\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\\n  documented under \\\"Cadence\\\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\\n  Its results are a\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\n\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\n\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\\n\\\"free laptop\\\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\\n\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\n\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\\n**Dart quick tier\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\n\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\n\\\"Patch does not apply\\\" or \\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\".\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\n\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\n\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\"run tests remotely\\nover SSH\\\" to \\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\": a heavy implementation or\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\n\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\nreference):\\n\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\"Get the code there\\\".\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\"$(mise env -s\\n   bash)\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\n   inside the same command.\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\n\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git rev-parse HEAD && /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git show -s --format='%H %P' ff8b980a && /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git diff --stat adb6af80..ff8b980a && /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git diff adb6af80..ff8b980a\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git rev-parse HEAD && /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git show -s --format='%H %P' ff8b980a && /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git diff --stat adb6af80..ff8b980a && /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git diff adb6af80..ff8b980a\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"ff8b980a24adface49198c5ae8710b43fe37fd5b\\nff8b980a24adface49198c5ae8710b43fe37fd5b adb6af802358829d50737ce5075e38654d34ce66\\n src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc             |  20 +++\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md    |   4 +-\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc      |  49 +++++--\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc       |  26 +++-\\n src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc      |  36 ++++-\\n test/yin/vm/provenance_test.cljc | 284 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\\n 6 files changed, 401 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\\nindex 1bb7ca84..43485ea7 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\\n@@ -121,6 +121,26 @@\\n   (contains? reserved-names x))\\n \\n \\n+(def reserved-prefix\\n+  \\\"The compiler-private name prefix (`dsg__`). Every name it begins is a\\n+   host or compiler temporary, never a guest name: the provenance doors\\n+   (`cell/dsg__new-provenance`, `cell/dsg__provenance`) and the Clojure\\n+   frontend's own temporaries. Source admission and macro admission refuse\\n+   it in any executable position — a variable, a binder, or a definition\\n+   target. A quoted symbol carrying it stays inert data.\\\"\\n+  \\\"dsg__\\\")\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn reserved-prefix-name?\\n+  \\\"True when `x` is a symbol whose local name begins with `reserved-prefix`,\\n+   whatever its namespace: a compiler-private name no guest code may spell\\n+   or construct.\\\"\\n+  [x]\\n+  (and (symbol? x)\\n+       (= reserved-prefix\\n+          (subs (name x) 0 (min (count (name x)) (count reserved-prefix))))))\\n+\\n+\\n (defn reserved-name-defect\\n   \\\"The data of a Rule R refusal: `role` names where the reserved name\\n    appeared (`:variable`, `:binder`, `:store-key`, `:definition-key`,\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md b/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md\\nindex 316535ff..1bb10d38 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/state.md\\n@@ -116,10 +116,12 @@ The state is an immutable map that contains all information needed to continue e\\n \\n ### `:heap` and `:gc` - Task Heap and Its Collector\\n \\n-**Type:** `:heap` is `{cell-id {:value v :seal s}}`; `:gc` is `{:since n :base b :threshold t :pinned #{cell-id}}`\\n+**Type:** `:heap` is `{cell-id {:value v :seal s :provenance p}}`; `:gc` is `{:since n :base b :threshold t :pinned #{cell-id}}`\\n \\n **Purpose:** `:heap` holds the task's cells, the mutable locations `cell/new` allocates and a sealed `:cell-ref` reaches. It is VM state, not continuation state: a write is seen by every holder of the ref and survives continuation re-entry (box semantics). `:gc` is its collector's state. Both are declared record fields wherever a VM is a positional record, and both start empty in every constructor and `spawn-module` child.\\n \\n+**Provenance:** an entry may carry an optional private `:provenance` sibling beside `:value` and `:seal`, written only by the privileged mint (`cell/dsg__new-provenance`, effect `:cell/new-provenance`) at allocation, never attached, replaced, or cleared afterwards. The VM treats it as opaque data — no frontend dispatch, no reason matching, no rendering — and answers it only through the authenticated private reader (`cell/dsg__provenance`, effect `:cell/provenance`), which returns nil for anything else and is never fatal. `cell/get` returns only `:value`; `cell/set!` and `heap-write` preserve `:provenance` while updating `:value`/`:seal`. The collector traces `:provenance` as data from every live entry, from pinned entries, and as an extra root at allocation, so payload cells referenced only by a retained provenance stay reachable while the wrapper is, and become collectible when the wrapper drops.\\n+\\n **Reclamation:** deterministic, allocation-triggered, stop-the-world mark-sweep, tracing kernel positions and value positions in two modes (`ast.md` Part 6),, a pure `vm -> vm'` (`yin.vm.engine/collect`). `:since` counts allocations; when it reaches `:threshold`, the `:cell/new` effect collects before allocating, and the threshold becomes `max(:base, 2 x live)`. `:base` is a composition parameter (`:gc-threshold`, default 4096). Marking runs over the heap as it was when the cycle began, and the sweep removes only ids of that snapshot, so an id allocated at or after the cycle's counter start is spared; a budgeted marker can later reuse it. `:pinned` holds cells whose refs left the VM's view on a stream or in an FFI request; they stay live for the task's life. Ids are never reused (`:id-counter` only grows), and a ref to a swept cell is refused with `:dead-or-forged-reference`. This supersedes the cell ruling's \\\"heap reclamation can wait\\\".\\n \\n ### `:continuation` - Return Context\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\\nindex 481436bd..e2c7a416 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\\n@@ -2443,9 +2443,11 @@\\n \\n (defn- heap-write\\n   \\\"`state` with heap cell `id` holding `v` under `seal`: allocation when the\\n-   cell is new, a write (the seal unchanged) when it is live.\\\"\\n+   cell is new, a write (the seal unchanged) when it is live. A write\\n+   preserves the entry's `:provenance` sibling; only the mint attaches one,\\n+   and only at allocation.\\\"\\n   [state id seal v]\\n-  (assoc-in state [:heap id] {:value v, :seal seal}))\\n+  (update-in state [:heap id] (fn [entry] (assoc entry :value v, :seal seal))))\\n \\n \\n ;; =============================================================================\\n@@ -2576,7 +2578,8 @@\\n                              (children-of x))\\n                   work (cond-> work\\n                          (and new-id? follow?)\\n-                         (conj [:data (:value (get heap id))])\\n+                         (-> (conj [:data (:value (get heap id))])\\n+                             (conj [:data (:provenance (get heap id))]))\\n                          (values/host-typed? x)\\n                          (conj [:kernel (values/payload x)])\\n                          (some? children)\\n@@ -2614,12 +2617,16 @@\\n \\n (defn- mark\\n   \\\"The ids of `heap` live from `roots` and from the `pinned` ids: a pinned\\n-   cell is a root, so its content is traced too, as data.\\\"\\n+   cell is a root, so its content — value and provenance — is traced too,\\n+   as data.\\\"\\n   [vm heap roots pinned]\\n   (let [pins (filterv #(contains? heap %) pinned)]\\n     (trace vm\\n            heap\\n-           (update roots :data into (map #(:value (get heap %))) pins)\\n+           (update roots :data\\n+                   into (mapcat (fn [id] (let [e (get heap id)]\\n+                                           [(:value e) (:provenance e)]))\\n+                                pins))\\n            (set pins)\\n            true)))\\n \\n@@ -2658,13 +2665,14 @@\\n \\n (defn- count-allocation\\n   \\\"`state` with one allocation counted, collected first when the count\\n-   reaches the threshold. `v`, the value being boxed, is held by no\\n-   register yet, so it is traced as an extra root.\\\"\\n-  [state v]\\n+   reaches the threshold. `roots`, the values being boxed (the content, and\\n+   for a mint the provenance), are held by no register yet, so each is\\n+   traced as an extra root.\\\"\\n+  [state & roots]\\n   (let [gc (gc-of state)\\n         since (inc (:since gc))]\\n     (if (>= since (:threshold gc))\\n-      (collect state [v])\\n+      (collect state (vec roots))\\n       (assoc state :gc (assoc gc :since since)))))\\n \\n \\n@@ -2797,6 +2805,29 @@\\n           {:state state,\\n            :value (authentic-ref? state :cell-ref (:cell effect)),\\n            :blocked? false}\\n+          ;; The privileged mint: a fresh sealed cell with `:value` and a\\n+          ;; private `:provenance` sibling installed atomically. Provenance\\n+          ;; is opaque to the engine; it is answered only by `:cell/provenance`.\\n+          :cell/new-provenance\\n+          (let [[id s'] (gensym (count-allocation state\\n+                                                  (:val effect)\\n+                                                  (:provenance effect))\\n+                                \\\"cell\\\")\\n+                ref (issue-ref s' :cell-ref id)]\\n+            {:state (assoc-in s' [:heap id] {:value (:val effect),\\n+                                             :seal (:seal ref),\\n+                                             :provenance (:provenance effect)}),\\n+             :value ref,\\n+             :blocked? false})\\n+          ;; The private reader: authenticate before consulting the slot, so\\n+          ;; an unauthenticated candidate answers nil instead of refusing.\\n+          ;; Never fatal, never mutating.\\n+          :cell/provenance\\n+          (let [ref (:cell effect)]\\n+            {:state state,\\n+             :value (when (authentic-ref? state :cell-ref ref)\\n+                      (get-in state [:heap (:id ref) :provenance])),\\n+             :blocked? false})\\n           (let [[id s'] (gensym state \\\"effect\\\")\\n                 handler (module/get-effect-handler (:modules state)\\n                                                    (:effect effect))]\\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\nindex d412d159..80ceccd9 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\n@@ -568,6 +568,18 @@\\n   (throw (ex-info msg (assoc data :yin.macro/prelude true))))\\n \\n \\n+(defn- check-guest-name!\\n+  \\\"Refuse `sym`, a guest-supplied name in `op`'s position, when it is\\n+   compiler-private (`dsg__`-prefixed): such names belong to the host and\\n+   compiler — the provenance doors and the frontend temporaries — so a macro\\n+   may construct no executable reference, binder, or definition target\\n+   carrying one. A quoted symbol stays inert data.\\\"\\n+  [op sym]\\n+  (when (vm/reserved-prefix-name? sym)\\n+    (throw-prelude (str op \\\" refuses the compiler-private name \\\" sym)\\n+                   {:op op, :name sym})))\\n+\\n+\\n (defn- packet?\\n   [x]\\n   (and (vector? x) (= 2 (count x)) (jing/segment-address? (first x))\\n@@ -808,6 +820,7 @@\\n   ([params body lineage]\\n    (when-not (and (sequential? params) (every? symbol? params))\\n      (throw-prelude \\\"yin/lambda params must be symbols\\\" {:op 'yin/lambda}))\\n+   (doseq [p params] (check-guest-name! 'yin/lambda p))\\n    (construct [:lambda (vec params) (first body)] [body] lineage)))\\n \\n \\n@@ -948,6 +961,7 @@\\n         'yin/variable (fn [sym]\\n                         (when-not (symbol? sym)\\n                           (throw-prelude \\\"yin/variable expects a symbol\\\" {:op 'yin/variable}))\\n+                        (check-guest-name! 'yin/variable sym)\\n                         (construct [:variable sym] [] lineage)),\\n         'yin/lambda (fn [params body]\\n                       (lambda-packet params (need-packet 'yin/lambda body)\\n@@ -973,11 +987,13 @@\\n                                             (need-packet 'yin/make-lambda body)\\n                                             lineage))),\\n         'yin/make-def (fn [name-tree value-tree]\\n-                        (application-packet\\n-                          (construct [:variable 'yin/def] [] lineage)\\n-                          [(literal-packet (name-of name-tree) lineage)\\n-                           (need-packet 'yin/make-def value-tree)]\\n-                          lineage)),\\n+                        (let [n (name-of name-tree)]\\n+                          (check-guest-name! 'yin/make-def n)\\n+                          (application-packet\\n+                            (construct [:variable 'yin/def] [] lineage)\\n+                            [(literal-packet n lineage)\\n+                             (need-packet 'yin/make-def value-tree)]\\n+                            lineage))),\\n         'yin/name-of name-of,\\n         'yin/gensym-sym gensym}))))\\n \\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\\nindex 0bc916ad..87ae5268 100644\\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc\\n@@ -410,9 +410,33 @@\\n   (make-effect :cell/authentic? {:cell c}))\\n \\n \\n+(defn new-provenance-cell\\n+  \\\"The privileged mint: allocate a fresh sealed cell holding `value` with\\n+   `provenance` installed in its private `:provenance` sibling, atomically.\\n+   `provenance` must be non-nil and is otherwise opaque to the VM. Only the\\n+   mint attaches provenance, and only at allocation. Not a guest surface:\\n+   the name is compiler-private (`dsg__`).\\\"\\n+  [value provenance]\\n+  (make-effect :cell/new-provenance {:val value, :provenance provenance}))\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn provenance-cell\\n+  \\\"The private reader: the `:provenance` a live, authenticated, marked cell\\n+   carries, or nil for anything else — an unmarked cell, a forged or dead\\n+   reference, or a non-reference. Never fatal, never mutating, and the\\n+   engine learns no frontend semantics from the slot. Not a guest surface:\\n+   the name is compiler-private (`dsg__`).\\\"\\n+  [candidate]\\n+  (make-effect :cell/provenance {:cell candidate}))\\n+\\n+\\n (def cell-module\\n-  \\\"The `cell` module definition. Registering it is a composition step.\\\"\\n-  {'new new-cell, 'get get-cell, 'set! set-cell!, 'authentic? authentic-cell?})\\n+  \\\"The `cell` module definition. Registering it is a composition step. The\\n+   two `dsg__` bindings are the compiler-private provenance mint and reader:\\n+   guest code may neither spell nor construct their names.\\\"\\n+  {'new new-cell, 'get get-cell, 'set! set-cell!, 'authentic? authentic-cell?,\\n+   'dsg__new-provenance new-provenance-cell,\\n+   'dsg__provenance provenance-cell})\\n \\n \\n (def cell-profiles\\n@@ -422,7 +446,13 @@\\n    'get (vm/primitive-profile 'get :effectful [1] #{:cell/get} :none)\\n    'set! (vm/primitive-profile 'set! :effectful [2] #{:cell/set!} :none)\\n    'authentic? (vm/primitive-profile 'authentic? :effectful [1]\\n-                                     #{:cell/authentic?} :none)})\\n+                                     #{:cell/authentic?} :none)\\n+   'dsg__new-provenance (vm/primitive-profile 'dsg__new-provenance\\n+                                              :effectful [2]\\n+                                              #{:cell/new-provenance} :none)\\n+   'dsg__provenance (vm/primitive-profile 'dsg__provenance\\n+                                          :effectful [1]\\n+                                          #{:cell/provenance} :none)})\\n \\n \\n (defn register-cell-module\\ndiff --git a/test/yin/vm/provenance_test.cljc b/test/yin/vm/provenance_test.cljc\\nnew file mode 100644\\nindex 00000000..6ebdf862\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/test/yin/vm/provenance_test.cljc\\n@@ -0,0 +1,284 @@\\n+(ns yin.vm.provenance-test\\n+  \\\"The VM provenance primitive (sol-approved, amended): a private\\n+   :provenance sibling on heap entries, written once by the privileged mint\\n+   (cell/dsg__new-provenance, effect :cell/new-provenance) and read only\\n+   by the authenticated private reader (cell/dsg__provenance, effect\\n+   :cell/provenance). The VM treats the slot as opaque data and answers it\\n+   for nothing but a live, authenticated, marked cell. Unit tests run on all\\n+   four VMs; the macro tests exercise the expander prelude.\\\"\\n+  (:require [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as sr-linearize]\\n+            [yin.vm.semantic.derive :as derive]\\n+            [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\n+            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n+            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as walker]\\n+            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\\n+            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\\n+            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\\n+            [yin.vm.macro :as m]\\n+            [yin.vm.module :as module]\\n+            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\n+            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]))\\n+\\n+\\n+(def ^:private opts\\n+  {:make-stream tu/make-stream,\\n+   :capability-secret tu/secret,\\n+   :primitives vm/primitives,\\n+   :modules (module/register-cell-module (module/default-registry))})\\n+\\n+\\n+(def ^:private images\\n+  {:ast-walker vm/ast->semantic-bytecode,\\n+   :semantic (fn [ast]\\n+               (:vector (sr-linearize/project-rows (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))),\\n+   :stack (fn [ast] (:image (derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))),\\n+   :register (fn [ast] (derive/register-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))})\\n+\\n+\\n+(def ^:private builders\\n+  {:ast-walker (fn [ast o]\\n+                 (walker/vm-load-rows (walker/create-vm o)\\n+                                      (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)\\n+                                      vm/ast-contract)),\\n+   :semantic (fn [ast o]\\n+               (semantic/load-vector (semantic/create-vm o)\\n+                                     ((:semantic images) ast)\\n+                                     vm/semantic-contract)),\\n+   :stack (fn [ast o]\\n+            (dvm/create-vm ((:stack images) ast)\\n+                           (assoc o :contract vm/stack-contract))),\\n+   :register (fn [ast o]\\n+               (rvm/create-vm ((:register images) ast)\\n+                              (assoc o :contract vm/register-contract)))})\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- run-on\\n+  [k ast threshold]\\n+  (vm/run ((get builders k) ast (assoc opts :gc-threshold threshold))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- on-every-vm\\n+  ([ast] (on-every-vm ast opts))\\n+  ([ast o]\\n+   (into {}\\n+         (map (fn [k]\\n+                [k (try (run-on k ast (get o :gc-threshold 1000000000))\\n+                        (catch #?(:clj Exception :cljs :default :cljd Object)\\n+                               e\\n+                          [:thrown (ex-data e)]))]))\\n+         (keys builders))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- every-vm=\\n+  [expected ast]\\n+  (doseq [threshold [1 1000000000]\\n+          [k result] (on-every-vm ast (assoc opts :gc-threshold threshold))]\\n+    (is (= expected (if (vector? result) result (vm/value result)))\\n+        (str k \\\" at gc threshold \\\" threshold))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- refusal-of\\n+  [thunk]\\n+  (try (thunk)\\n+       nil\\n+       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\\n+         (or (ex-data e) {}))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- lit [x] {:type :literal, :value x})\\n+(defn- v [sym] {:type :variable, :name sym})\\n+(defn- lam [params body] {:type :lambda, :params params, :body body})\\n+(defn- app [op & args] {:type :application, :operator op, :operands (vec args)})\\n+(defn- then [a b] (app (lam ['_] b) a))\\n+(defn- let1 [sym init body] (app (lam [sym] body) init))\\n+(defn- pair [a b] (app (v 'conj) (app (v 'conj) (lit []) a) b))\\n+(defn- new-cell [x] (app (v 'cell/new) x))\\n+(defn- get-cell [c] (app (v 'cell/get) c))\\n+(defn- set-cell! [c x] (app (v 'cell/set!) c x))\\n+(defn- new-provenance-cell [value provenance]\\n+  (app (v 'cell/dsg__new-provenance) value provenance))\\n+(defn- provenance-cell [candidate]\\n+  (app (v 'cell/dsg__provenance) candidate))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest mint-installs-provenance-atomically-test\\n+  (every-vm= {:retained 1}\\n+             (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                   (provenance-cell (v 'c)))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest an-ordinary-cell-carries-no-provenance-test\\n+  (every-vm= nil\\n+             (let1 'c (new-cell (lit 42))\\n+                   (provenance-cell (v 'c)))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest provenance-is-hidden-from-get-test\\n+  (every-vm= 42\\n+             (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                   (get-cell (v 'c)))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest set!-preserves-provenance-test\\n+  (every-vm= {:retained 1}\\n+             (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                   (then (set-cell! (v 'c) (lit 99))\\n+                         (provenance-cell (v 'c)))))\\n+  (every-vm= 99\\n+             (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                   (then (set-cell! (v 'c) (lit 99))\\n+                         (get-cell (v 'c))))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest content-copy-loses-provenance-test\\n+  (testing \\\"copying the visible content into a fresh cell mints no provenance\\\"\\n+    (every-vm= [42 nil]\\n+               (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                     (let1 'copy (new-cell (get-cell (v 'c)))\\n+                           (pair (get-cell (v 'copy))\\n+                                 (provenance-cell (v 'copy))))))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest ref-copy-retains-rethrow-authority-test\\n+  (testing \\\"copying the sealed reference still names the same marked cell\\\"\\n+    (every-vm= {:retained 1}\\n+               (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                     (let1 'alias (v 'c)\\n+                           (provenance-cell (v 'alias)))))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest provenance-query-is-never-fatal-test\\n+  (testing \\\"a forged ref answers nil instead of refusing\\\"\\n+    (every-vm= nil\\n+               (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                     (provenance-cell (app (v 'assoc) (v 'c) (lit :seal)\\n+                                           (lit \\\"x\\\"))))))\\n+  (testing \\\"a dead id answers nil\\\"\\n+    (every-vm= nil\\n+               (let1 'c (new-provenance-cell (lit 42) (lit {:retained 1}))\\n+                     (provenance-cell (app (v 'assoc) (v 'c) (lit :id)\\n+                                           (lit :cell-999))))))\\n+  (testing \\\"a non-reference answers nil\\\"\\n+    (every-vm= nil (provenance-cell (lit 5)))\\n+    (every-vm= nil (provenance-cell (lit {:type :cell-ref})))))\\n+\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- quiesce\\n+  [vm]\\n+  (assoc vm\\n+         :value nil\\n+         :control nil\\n+         :env {}\\n+         :k nil\\n+         :stack []\\n+         :registers []\\n+         :frames []\\n+         :continuation []))\\n+\\n+\\n+(defn- minted-wrapper\\n+  [k]\\n+  (let [result (run-on k (new-cell (lit :payload)) 1000000)\\n+        payload-ref (vm/value result)\\n+        q (quiesce result)\\n+        {:keys [state value]}\\n+        (engine/handle-effect q (module/make-effect :cell/new-provenance\\n+                                                     {:val :visible\\n+                                                      :provenance payload-ref})\\n+                              {})]\\n+    [(quiesce state) value payload-ref]))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest provenance-only-payload-survives-ordinary-collection-test\\n+  (doseq [k (keys builders)]\\n+    (let [[state wrapper-ref payload-ref] (minted-wrapper k)\\n+          rooted (assoc-in state [:store 'w] wrapper-ref)\\n+          collected (engine/collect rooted)]\\n+      (testing (str k)\\n+        (is (contains? (:heap collected) (:id wrapper-ref)))\\n+        (is (contains? (:heap collected) (:id payload-ref))\\n+            \\\"the payload survives though only provenance names it\\\")))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest pinned-wrapper-traces-its-provenance-test\\n+  (doseq [k (keys builders)]\\n+    (let [[state wrapper-ref payload-ref] (minted-wrapper k)\\n+          pinned (assoc-in state [:gc :pinned] #{(:id wrapper-ref)})\\n+          collected (engine/collect pinned)]\\n+      (testing (str k)\\n+        (is (contains? (:heap collected) (:id wrapper-ref)))\\n+        (is (contains? (:heap collected) (:id payload-ref))\\n+            \\\"the pinned wrapper provenance roots the payload\\\")))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest mint-held-provenance-survives-its-own-collection-test\\n+  (doseq [k (keys builders)]\\n+    (let [result (run-on k (new-cell (lit :payload)) 1000000)\\n+          payload-ref (vm/value result)\\n+          q (quiesce result)\\n+          q (assoc q :gc (assoc (:gc q) :since 0 :threshold 1))\\n+          {:keys [state]}\\n+          (engine/handle-effect q (module/make-effect :cell/new-provenance\\n+                                                       {:val :visible\\n+                                                        :provenance payload-ref})\\n+                                {})]\\n+      (testing (str k)\\n+        (is (zero? (:since (:gc state))) \\\"the mint allocation collected\\\")\\n+        (is (contains? (:heap state) (:id payload-ref))\\n+            \\\"the provenance was held as an extra root through the\\n+             allocation-triggered collection\\\")))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest dropping-the-wrapper-reclaims-the-cluster-test\\n+  (doseq [k (keys builders)]\\n+    (let [[state wrapper-ref payload-ref] (minted-wrapper k)\\n+          dropped (engine/collect state)]\\n+      (testing (str k)\\n+        (is (not (contains? (:heap dropped) (:id wrapper-ref))))\\n+        (is (not (contains? (:heap dropped) (:id payload-ref)))\\n+            \\\"the payload cell reachable only through provenance is collected\\n+             once its wrapper is\\\")))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest exported-helper-refuses-the-doors-test\\n+  (let [prelude (m/prelude identity)\\n+        variable (get prelude 'yin/variable)\\n+        lambda (get prelude 'yin/lambda)\\n+        make-def (get prelude 'yin/make-def)\\n+        body (m/ast->packet (lit 1))]\\n+    (testing \\\"yin/variable - the reference helper\\\"\\n+      (doseq [door '[cell/dsg__new-provenance cell/dsg__provenance]]\\n+        (let [d (refusal-of #(variable door))]\\n+          (is (= true (:yin.macro/prelude d)) (str door))\\n+          (is (= door (:name d)) (str door)))))\\n+    (testing \\\"yin/lambda - the binder helper\\\"\\n+      (let [d (refusal-of #(lambda ['cell/dsg__provenance] body))]\\n+        (is (= true (:yin.macro/prelude d)))\\n+        (is (= 'cell/dsg__provenance (:name d)))))\\n+    (testing \\\"yin/make-def - the definition-target helper\\\"\\n+      (let [name-tree (m/ast->packet (lit 'cell/dsg__provenance))\\n+            d (refusal-of #(make-def name-tree body))]\\n+        (is (= true (:yin.macro/prelude d)))\\n+        (is (= 'cell/dsg__provenance (:name d)))))\\n+    (testing \\\"a quoted name is inert data, not a refusal\\\"\\n+      (is (nil? (refusal-of\\n+                  #(m/invoke (m/macro-entry\\n+                               (m/ast->packet\\n+                                 (lam [] (app (v 'yin/literal)\\n+                                              (lit 'cell/dsg__new-provenance))))\\n+                               vm/ast-contract)\\n+                             []\\n+                             (m/make-ctx {:token \\\"t\\\"}))))))))\\n+\\n+\\n+(deftest macro-cannot-construct-the-mint-door-test\\n+  (testing \\\"a macro body that builds the door as a variable for indirect\\n+            application is refused at invocation, not at expansion\\\"\\n+    (let [macro-ast (lam []\\n+                         (app (v 'yin/variable)\\n+                              (lit 'cell/dsg__new-provenance)))\\n+          entry (m/macro-entry (m/ast->packet macro-ast) vm/ast-contract)\\n+          d (refusal-of #(m/invoke entry [] (m/make-ctx {:token \\\"t\\\"})))]\\n+      (is (= :body-error (:kind d)))\\n+      (is (re-find #\\\"dsg__new-provenance\\\" (str (:message d)))))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The commit is available in the sibling `datomworld-vmprov` checkout. It has the stated parent and six changed files, with one filename discrepancy: the new test is `provenance_test.cljc`, not `.clj`. The three GC changes are present; I’m checking root isolation in the tests and whether macro admission closes every construction path.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc; sed -n '260,565p' src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc; sed -n '580,1020p' src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc; sed -n '260,565p' src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc; sed -n '580,1020p' src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(ns yin.vm.macro\\n  \\\"The row-native macro expander (`docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md`, Phase 1).\\n\\n   A process between two media: it observes `program-in` batches\\n   `[:yin.program/batch trees run-index declaration-rows harvest-rows\\n   call-site-claims]` — the trailing element present only when some\\n   member's frontend claimed call-site (C1) coverage, holding one\\n   boolean verdict per member, so every older five-element batch is\\n   still a valid unclaimed batch —\\n   rewrites the selected tree to a fixpoint, and appends one canonical tree\\n   packet `[root rows]` to `program-out`, with one event row per attempt on\\n   an optional log medium. Evaluators know nothing of any of this (decision\\n   1): a macro call is an ordinary `:application` row, macro-ness is an\\n   occurrence declaration beside the tree, and definitions leave as ordinary\\n   syntax or as a literal naming the lowered macro.\\n\\n   Everything here is a value threaded through the step: the macro store,\\n   attempt counter, batch coordinate, and incarnation token live in `ctx`\\n   (§3.1). Expansion failure is data (decision 12); a throw is a defect or a\\n   terminal stream outcome.\\\"\\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [flush load])\\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\\n            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as ast-walker]\\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Grammar, rows, and packets (§1)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def ^:private standin-tag :yin.macro/defined)\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private working-grammar\\n  \\\"The canonical §2.3 table plus the one working row the expander interns\\n   and lowers itself: `[S :yin.macro/defined name k]` (§3.2 step 3). It is\\n   admitted only in operand and result packets, never in input or output.\\\"\\n  (assoc vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar standin-tag [[:name :sym] [:k :int]]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- grammar\\n  [working?]\\n  (if working? working-grammar vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar))\\n\\n\\n(defn- row-tag\\n  [row]\\n  (nth row 1))\\n\\n\\n(defn- intern-row\\n  \\\"The full row for `body`: its content address prepended (§1.1).\\\"\\n  [body]\\n  (into [(jing/segment-key body)] body))\\n\\n\\n(defn- slot-kinds\\n  \\\"`[[position kind] ...]` for a tag's grammar slots, positions being full-row\\n   positions (address 0, tag 1, first slot 2).\\\"\\n  [tag]\\n  (map-indexed (fn [i [_ kind]] [(+ i 2) kind]) (get working-grammar tag)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- child-places\\n  \\\"`[[coordinate child-address] ...]` of one row in grammar order: a `node`\\n   slot's coordinate is its full-row position, a `nodes` child's is\\n   `[position i]` (§2.1).\\\"\\n  [row]\\n  (mapcat (fn [[pos kind]]\\n            (case kind\\n              :node [[pos (nth row pos)]]\\n              :nodes (map-indexed (fn [i c] [[pos i] c]) (nth row pos))\\n              nil))\\n          (slot-kinds (row-tag row))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- with-child\\n  \\\"`row`'s body with the child at coordinate `coord` replaced by `address`.\\\"\\n  [row coord address]\\n  (let [body (subvec row 1)]\\n    (if (vector? coord)\\n      (let [[pos i] coord]\\n        (assoc body (dec pos) (assoc (nth row pos) i address)))\\n      (assoc body (dec coord) address))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- reachable\\n  \\\"Addresses reachable from `root` through `index`, first-encounter preorder\\n   in grammar order. Assumes the index is closed and acyclic.\\\"\\n  [index root]\\n  (loop [stack (list root)\\n         seen #{}\\n         order []]\\n    (if-let [a (first stack)]\\n      (if (contains? seen a)\\n        (recur (rest stack) seen order)\\n        (recur (reduce conj (rest stack) (reverse (map second (child-places (get index a)))))\\n               (conj seen a)\\n               (conj order a)))\\n      order)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- packet-of\\n  \\\"The self-contained tree packet `[root rows]` rooted at `root` (§1.2). Row\\n   order is preorder, which is deterministic but carries no meaning.\\\"\\n  [index root]\\n  [root (mapv #(get index %) (reachable index root))])\\n\\n\\n(defn tree-packet\\n  \\\"Convert a projected row set `{:root id :rows {id row}}` (the shape\\n   `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` returns) to a tree packet `[root rows]`.\\\"\\n  [{:keys [root rows]}]\\n  (packet-of rows root))\\n\\n\\n(defn packet->row-set\\n  \\\"Convert a canonical tree packet to `{:root id :rows {id row}}`, the shape\\n   the evaluator loaders take.\\\"\\n  [[root rows]]\\n  {:root root, :rows (into {} (map (fn [r] [(first r) r])) rows)})\\n\\n\\n(defn ast->packet\\n  \\\"Project a map AST to its canonical tree packet.\\\"\\n  [ast]\\n  (tree-packet (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- same-value?\\n  \\\"`=` that also compares metadata at every depth: the transitional address\\n   encoder does not hash scalar metadata, so two bodies may share an address\\n   while differing there, which is an address conflict (§5.3, §7.2).\\\"\\n  [a b]\\n  (and (= a b)\\n       (= (meta a) (meta b))\\n       (or (nil? (meta a)) (same-value? (meta a) (meta b)))\\n       (cond (map? a) (every? (fn [[k v]]\\n                                (let [[k' v'] (find b k)]\\n                                  (and (same-value? k k') (same-value? v v'))))\\n                              a)\\n             (set? a) (every? (fn [e] (same-value? e (some #(when (= e %) %) b))) a)\\n             (sequential? a) (every? true? (map same-value? a b))\\n             :else true)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Packet validation (§7.1, §7.2)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- standin-shaped?\\n  \\\"A vector that reads as a working stand-in row or body.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (and (vector? x)\\n       (or (= standin-tag (first x))\\n           (and (< 1 (count x)) (= standin-tag (nth x 1))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- marker-in?\\n  \\\"True when a stand-in-shaped vector appears anywhere inside a value.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (or (standin-shaped? x)\\n      (and (map? x) (some (fn [[k v]] (or (marker-in? k) (marker-in? v))) x))\\n      (and (coll? x) (not (map? x)) (some marker-in? x))\\n      (and (some? (meta x)) (marker-in? (meta x)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- slot-shape-ok?\\n  \\\"Slot kind check. `data`/`key` admit any value here; plain-ness is the\\n   separate `:host-value` rule.\\\"\\n  [kind v]\\n  (case kind\\n    :node (jing/segment-address? v)\\n    :nodes (and (vector? v) (every? jing/segment-address? v))\\n    (:data :key) true\\n    :sym (symbol? v)\\n    :syms (and (vector? v) (every? symbol? v))\\n    :kw (keyword? v)\\n    :str (string? v)\\n    :int (and (integer? v) (not (neg? v)))\\n    :bool (or (true? v) (false? v))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- value-slots\\n  [row]\\n  (keep (fn [[pos kind]] (when (contains? #{:data :key} kind) (nth row pos)))\\n        (slot-kinds (row-tag row))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- first-defect\\n  \\\"Run `check` over `addresses` in order; the first non-nil result.\\\"\\n  [check addresses]\\n  (some check addresses))\\n\\n\\n(defn- cycle-defect\\n  [index addresses]\\n  (letfn [(visit\\n            [a on-path path done]\\n            (cond (contains? on-path a) [{:reason :cyclic, :address a, :path path} done]\\n                  (contains? done a) [nil done]\\n                  :else (let [[err done']\\n                              (reduce (fn [[_ d] [coord c]]\\n                                        (let [[e d'] (visit c (conj on-path a)\\n                                                            (conj path coord) d)]\\n                                          (if e (reduced [e d']) [nil d'])))\\n                                      [nil done]\\n                                      (child-places (get index a)))]\\n                          [err (conj done' a)])))]\\n    (loop [as addresses\\n           done #{}]\\n      (when-let [a (first as)]\\n        (let [[err done'] (visit a #{} [] done)]\\n          (or err (recur (rest as) done')))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn valid-tree?\\n  \\\"Validate a tree packet `[root rows]` (§1.2, §7.1). Returns nil when the\\n   packet is a closed, self-contained, acyclic tree of well-formed rows whose\\n   addresses match their bodies, else the first error as\\n   `{:kind :malformed-input :reason r :address a ...}`.\\n\\n   Rules run in this order, each over addresses in canonical byte order and\\n   slots in grammar order: packet and row shape (`:packet-shape`,\\n   `:row-shape`), `:unknown-tag`, `:row-shape` (arity and slot kinds),\\n   `:host-value`, `:marker-in-payload`, `:address-mismatch` (after the plain\\n   check, so no host value is ever hashed), `:address-conflict` (one address,\\n   two metadata-distinct bodies), `:dangling-child`, `:cyclic`, and\\n   `:unreachable-row`.\\n\\n   `opts`: `:working?` admits working stand-in rows (operand and result\\n   packets); canonical input and output never do.\\\"\\n  ([packet] (valid-tree? packet {}))\\n  ([packet {:keys [working?]}]\\n   (let [g (grammar working?)\\n         err (fn [reason & kvs]\\n               (merge {:kind :malformed-input, :reason reason}\\n                      (apply hash-map kvs)))]\\n     (if-not (and (vector? packet) (= 2 (count packet))\\n                  (jing/segment-address? (nth packet 0))\\n                  (sequential? (nth packet 1)))\\n       (err :packet-shape)\\n       (let [[root rows] packet]\\n         (or\\n           (some (fn [row]\\n                   (when-not (and (vector? row) (<= 2 (count row))\\n                                  (jing/segment-address? (nth row 0)))\\n                     (err :row-shape :address (when (vector? row) (first row)))))\\n                 rows)\\n           (let [by-addr (group-by first rows)\\n                 addresses (sort-by name (keys by-addr))\\n                 index (into {} (map (fn [[a rs]] [a (first rs)])) by-addr)]\\n             (or\\n               (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                               (when-not (contains? g (row-tag (get index a)))\\n                                 (err :unknown-tag :address a)))\\n                             addresses)\\n                             addresses)\\n               (first-defect\\n                 (fn [a]\\n                   (let [row (get index a)\\n                         slots (get g (row-tag row))]\\n                     (when-not (and (= (count row) (+ 2 (count slots)))\\n                                    (every? (fn [[i [_ kind]]]\\n                                              (slot-shape-ok? kind (nth row (+ i 2))))\\n                                            (map-indexed vector slots)))\\n                       (err :row-shape :address a))))\\n                 addresses)\\n               (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                               (when-not (every? vm/plain-data? (get by-addr a))\\n                                 (err :host-value :address a)))\\n                             addresses)\\n               (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                               (when (some marker-in? (value-slots (get index a)))\\n                                 (err :marker-in-payload :address a)))\\n                             addresses)\\n               (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                               (when-not (every? (fn [row]\\n                                                   (jing/segment-matches?\\n                                                     a\\n                                                     (subvec row 1)))\\n                                                 (get by-addr a))\\n                                 (err :address-mismatch :address a)))\\n                             addresses)\\n               (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                               (let [[r & more] (get by-addr a)]\\n                                 (when-not (every? #(same-value? r %) more)\\n                                   (err :address-conflict :address a))))\\n                             addresses)\\n               (when-not (contains? index root)\\n                 (err :dangling-child :address nil :child root))\\n               (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                               (some (fn [[_ c]]\\n                                       (when-not (contains? index c)\\n                                         (err :dangling-child :address a :child c)))\\n                                     (child-places (get index a))))\\n                             addresses)\\n               (when-let [{:keys [address path]} (cycle-defect index (cons root addresses))]\\n                 (err :cyclic :address address :path path))\\n               (let [seen (set (reachable index root))]\\n                 (first-defect (fn [a]\\n                                 (when-not (contains? seen a)\\n                                   (err :unreachable-row :address a)))\\n                               addresses))))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- packet-index\\n  [[_ rows]]\\n  (into {} (map (fn [r] [(first r) r])) rows))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Paths and definitions (§2.1, §2.2)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- step-path\\n  \\\"The child address at one path coordinate of `row`, or nil when the\\n   coordinate names no `node`/`nodes` child of it.\\\"\\n  [row coord]\\n  (let [kinds (into {} (slot-kinds (row-tag row)))]\\n    (cond (integer? coord)\\n          (when (= :node (get kinds coord)) (nth row coord))\\n\\n          (and (vector? coord) (= 2 (count coord)))\\n          (let [[pos i] coord]\\n            (when (and (integer? pos) (integer? i) (= :nodes (get kinds pos)))\\n              (get (nth row pos) i)))\\n\\n          :else nil)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- resolve-path\\n  \\\"The address at `path` from `root`, or nil when the path does not resolve.\\\"\\n  [index root path]\\n  (when (vector? path)\\n    (reduce (fn [a coord]\\n              (or (step-path (get index a) coord) (reduced nil)))\\n            root\\n            path)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- definition-at\\n  \\\"`{:name sym :value address}` when the row at `address` is the §2.2 shape\\n   `(yin/def <literal-symbol> value)`, else nil.\\\"\\n  [index address]\\n  (let [row (get index address)]\\n    (when (= :application (row-tag row))\\n      (let [op (get index (nth row 2))\\n            operands (nth row 3)]\\n        (when (and (= [:variable 'yin/def] (subvec op 1))\\n                   (= 2 (count operands)))\\n          (let [name-row (get index (first operands))]\\n            (when (and (= :literal (row-tag name-row))\\n                       (symbol? (nth name-row 2)))\\n              {:name (nth name-row 2), :value (second operands)})))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- definition-paths\\n  \\\"Every definition occurrence path of one tree, in preorder. Paths, not\\n   addresses: a shared definition row at two places is two occurrences.\\\"\\n  [index root]\\n  (letfn [(walk\\n            [a path]\\n            (concat (when (definition-at index a) [path])\\n                    (mapcat (fn [[coord c]] (walk c (conj path coord)))\\n                            (child-places (get index a)))))]\\n    (vec (walk root []))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Admission (§2.1, §7.1)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- malformed\\n  [reason & kvs]\\n  (merge {:kind :malformed-input, :reason reason} (apply hash-map kvs)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- batch-shape-ok?\\n  [batch]\\n  (and (vector? batch)\\n       (<= 5 (count batch) 6)\\n       (= :yin.program/batch (nth batch 0))\\n       (let [[_ trees run-index decls harvest claim] batch]\\n         (and (vector? trees) (seq trees)\\n              (integer? run-index) (<= 0 run-index) (< run-index (count trees))\\n              (vector? decls)\\n              (every? #(and (vector? %) (= 3 (count %))\\n                            (= :yin.macro/definition (first %)))\\n                      decls)\\n              (vector? harvest)\\n              (every? #(and (vector? %) (= 4 (count %))\\n                            (= :yin.macro/harvest (first %)))\\n                      harvest)\\n              (or (nil? claim)\\n                  (and (vector? claim)\\n                       (= (count claim) (count trees))\\n                       (every? boolean? claim)))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- merge-indexes\\n  \\\"Union the trees' indexes into one working index, verifying that one\\n   address denotes one body across trees (each tree is already internally\\n   consistent). `{:index m}`, or `{:error e}` naming the first later tree, in\\n   tree order and then canonical address order, whose row is\\n   metadata-distinct from an earlier tree's row at the same address.\\\"\\n  [indexes]\\n  (reduce (fn [{:keys [index]} [j tree-index]]\\n            (or (some (fn [a]\\n                        (let [prior (get index a)]\\n                          (when (and prior (not (same-value? prior (get tree-index a))))\\n                            (reduced {:error (malformed :address-conflict\\n                                                        :tree j :address a)}))))\\n                      (sort-by name (keys tree-index)))\\n                {:index (merge index tree-index)}))\\n          {:index {}}\\n          (map-indexed vector indexes)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- admit\\n  \\\"Admit one batch: `{:admitted {...}}` or `{:error error-data}`. The first\\n   error is deterministic: batch shape, then trees by index, then\\n   cross-tree address conflicts, then catalogue problems by ordinal, then\\n   declarations in batch order.\\\"\\n  [batch]\\n  (if-not (batch-shape-ok? batch)\\n    {:error (malformed :batch-shape)}\\n    (let [[_ trees run-index decls harvest] batch]\\n      (or\\n        (some (fn [[j tree]]\\n                (when-let [e (valid-tree? tree)]\\n                  {:error (assoc e :tree j)}))\\n              (map-indexed vector trees))\\n        (let [indexes (mapv packet-index trees)\\n              merged (merge-indexes indexes)\\n              roots (mapv first trees)\\n              catalogue-error (fn [h & kvs] {:error (apply malformed :harvest-catalogue :ordinal h kvs)})\\n              entries\\n              (reduce\\n                (fn [acc [i [_ h j path]]]\\n                  (let [a (when (and (integer? j) (< -1 j (count trees)))\\n                            (resolve-path (nth indexes j) (nth roots j) path))\\n                        d (when a (definition-at (nth indexes j) a))]\\n                    (cond (not= i h) (reduced (catalogue-error h))\\n                          (nil? d) (reduced (catalogue-error h :tree j :path path))\\n                          (contains? (:seen acc) [j path])\\n                          (reduced (catalogue-error h :tree j :path path))\\n                          :else (-> acc\\n                                    (update :seen conj [j path])\\n                                    (update :entries conj\\n                                            (assoc d :ordinal h, :tree j, :path path\\n                                                   :address a))))))\\n                {:seen #{}, :entries []}\\n                (map-indexed vector harvest))]\\n          (cond\\n            (:error merged) merged\\n\\n            (:error entries) entries\\n\\n            :else\\n            (let [{:keys [seen entries]} entries]\\n              (or\\n                (some (fn [j]\\n                        (some (fn [p]\\n                                (when-not (contains? seen [j p])\\n                                  (catalogue-error nil :tree j :path p)))\\n                              (definition-paths (nth indexes j) (nth roots j))))\\n                      (range (count trees)))\\n                (let [by-occurrence (into {} (map (fn [e] [[(:tree e) (:path e)] e])) entries)]\\n                  (or\\n                    (some (fn [[_ j path]]\\n                            (let [e (get by-occurrence [j path])]\\n                              (when-not (and e (= :lambda (row-tag (get (nth indexes j)\\n                                                                        (:value e)))))\\n                                {:error (malformed :stray-macro-declaration :tree j :path path)})))\\n                          decls)\\n                    (let [declared (set (map (fn [[_ j path]] [j path]) decls))]\\n                      {:admitted\\n                       {:trees trees,\\n                        :indexes indexes,\\n                        :index (:index merged),\\n                        :run-index run-index,\\n                        :definitions (mapv #(assoc % :declared?\\n                                                   (contains? declared [(:tree %) (:path %)]))\\n                                           entries)}})))))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn definitions\\n  \\\"The ordered definition records of a batch, in harvest order:\\n   `{:ordinal h :tree j :path p :address a :name sym :value v :declared? b}`.\\n   Throws `ex-info` carrying the admission error when the batch is invalid.\\\"\\n  [batch]\\n  (let [{:keys [admitted error]} (admit batch)]\\n    (when error\\n      (throw (ex-info \\\"Invalid macro input batch\\\" error)))\\n    (:definitions admitted)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Workspace: interning and immutable path rebuilding (§3.3)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- intern-body\\n  \\\"Intern `body` into the working index: `[index' address]`. An address\\n   already holding a different body is a defect of the encoder, not input.\\\"\\n  [index body]\\n  (let [row (intern-row body)\\n        a (first row)\\n        prior (get index a)]\\n    (when (and prior (not (same-value? prior row)))\\n      (throw (ex-info \\\"Working index address conflict\\\" {:address a})))\\n    [(if prior index (assoc index a row)) a]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- rebuild-paths\\n  \\\"Replace the occurrences at every path in `replacements` (`{path address}`)\\n   below `root`, interning each changed ancestor once: replacements below one\\n   parent are combined before the parent is interned. Shallower replacements\\n   subsume deeper ones, which is the result of applying them deepest path\\n   first. Content sharing never merges occurrences: only the named paths'\\n   ancestor chains change. Returns `[index' root']`.\\\"\\n  [index root replacements]\\n  (letfn [(go\\n            [index a reps]\\n            (if-let [[_ r] (find reps [])]\\n              [index r]\\n              (let [by-coord (group-by (comp first key) reps)\\n                    row (get index a)\\n                    [index row']\\n                    (reduce (fn [[index row'] coord]\\n                              (let [child (step-path row coord)\\n                                    sub (into {} (map (fn [[p r]] [(subvec p 1) r]))\\n                                              (get by-coord coord))\\n                                    [index c'] (go index child sub)]\\n                                [index (into [a] (with-child row' coord c'))]))\\n                            [index row]\\n                            ;; deterministic order; the result is order-free\\n                            (sort-by pr-str (keys by-coord)))]\\n                (if (= row' row)\\n                  [index a]\\n                  (intern-body index (subvec row' 1))))))]\\n    (go index root replacements)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- rebuild-row\\n  \\\"Intern `row` with the children at `places` (a subsequence of its\\n   `child-places`, all of them by default) replaced by `children'`, in\\n   order; `[index address]`, unchanged when no child changed.\\\"\\n  ([index row children'] (rebuild-row index row (child-places row) children'))\\n  ([index row places children']\\n   (if (= (map second places) children')\\n     [index (first row)]\\n     (intern-body index (subvec (reduce (fn [r [[coord _] c]]\\n                                          (into [(first r)] (with-child r coord c)))\\n                                        row\\n                                        (map vector places children'))\\n                                1)))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Bounded body runner and row-native prelude (§5)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n                   {:op op, :name sym})))\\n\\n\\n(defn- packet?\\n  [x]\\n  (and (vector? x) (= 2 (count x)) (jing/segment-address? (first x))\\n       (vector? (second x))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- need-packet\\n  [who x]\\n  (when-not (packet? x)\\n    (throw-prelude (str who \\\" expects a tree packet\\\") {:op who}))\\n  x)\\n\\n\\n(defn- merge-packets\\n  \\\"Union the rows of `packets` into one index, verifying that one address\\n   denotes one body (§5.3).\\\"\\n  [packets]\\n  (reduce (fn [index row]\\n            (let [prior (get index (first row))]\\n              (cond (nil? prior) (assoc index (first row) row)\\n                    (same-value? prior row) index\\n                    :else (throw-prelude \\\"Packet address conflict\\\"\\n                                         {:address (first row)}))))\\n          {}\\n          (mapcat second packets)))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private pending-expansion-origin\\n  \\\"The construction origin the prelude attaches to every node the\\n   macro's code builds during one invocation (ruling U1: identity comes\\n   from the construction event, never from comparing content\\n   addresses). `expand-call` substitutes the recorded expansion event's\\n   `[:expansion record-address]` origin for it when the output's\\n   occurrence correspondence is read — the placeholder never reaches a\\n   table, a row, or an event it does not belong to.\\\"\\n  [:expansion ::pending])\\n\\n\\n(defn- with-origin-of\\n  \\\"Tag packet `p` with `f`, its construction origin function: `(f rel)`\\n   answers the origin of the occurrence at `rel`, a path relative to\\n   `p`'s root — the pending expansion origin for a node the macro's\\n   code constructed, or, below a child coordinate that retains a\\n   passed-through packet, that packet's own origin function. The\\n   function rides the packet's metadata, so it is content-addressing\\n   invisible and never leaks into a final tree.\\\"\\n  [p f]\\n  (vary-meta p assoc ::origin-of f))\\n\\n\\n(defn- origin-of-fn\\n  \\\"The construction origin function packet `p` carries, or nil.\\\"\\n  [p]\\n  (::origin-of (meta p)))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private tracking-meta-keys\\n  \\\"The private provenance entries the expander rides on packet values.\\n   They are host functions, so they must never enter a hashed or\\n   validated data slot (P1-2); every other metadata entry is the\\n   caller's legitimate data and is preserved.\\\"\\n  #{::origin-of})\\n\\n\\n(defn- strip-tracking\\n  \\\"Remove only the private tracking entries, recursively, from `x`:\\n   every collection element, map key and value, and every metadata map\\n   (including metadata on metadata) is visited, so a packet nested at\\n   any depth inside a literal payload loses its host-function origin\\n   while legitimate user metadata survives untouched.\\\"\\n  [x]\\n  (let [x' (cond (record? x) x\\n                 (map? x) (into (empty x)\\n                                (map (fn [[k v]]\\n                                       [(strip-tracking k)\\n                                        (strip-tracking v)]))\\n                                x)\\n                 (set? x) (into (empty x) (map strip-tracking) x)\\n                 (vector? x) (mapv strip-tracking x)\\n                 (sequential? x) (with-meta\\n                                   (apply list (map strip-tracking x))\\n                                   nil)\\n                 :else x)\\n        m (meta x)]\\n    (if m\\n      (let [m' (strip-tracking\\n                 (apply dissoc m tracking-meta-keys))]\\n        (with-meta x' (when-not (and (empty? m') (nil? (meta m'))) m')))\\n      x')))\\n\\n\\n(defn- packet-has-application?\\n  \\\"True when packet `p` contains an `:application` occurrence, the only\\n   occurrences whose provenance a correspondence ever reads.\\\"\\n  [p]\\n  (boolean (and (packet? p)\\n                (some #(= :application (row-tag %)) (second p)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- register-lineage!\\n  \\\"Record packet `p` in the invocation `lineage` (an atom of entries)\\n   under the identity of its rows vector. Host decomposition\\n   (`first`/`rest`/`get`) hands that very object to reassembly, so the\\n   entry is how a rebuilt packet proves it is this packet retained,\\n   never a content twin built anew. Returns `p`.\\\"\\n  [lineage p]\\n  (when (and lineage (packet? p) (origin-of-fn p))\\n    (when-not (some #(identical? (second p) (:rows %)) @lineage)\\n      (swap! lineage conj {:root (first p), :rows (second p),\\n                           :fn (origin-of-fn p)})))\\n  p)\\n\\n\\n(defn- lineage-origin-for\\n  \\\"The retained origin function for candidate packet `c`, or nil when\\n   no registered packet unambiguously accounts for it. Content\\n   equality alone never suffices — a freshly built twin holds equal\\n   but distinct row objects and recovers nothing here (ruling U1).\\n\\n   Exact container lineage is preferred: a registered packet whose\\n   rows vector is the very object `c` carries (host decomposition\\n   hands that object to reassembly) is occurrence-specific, so it\\n   wins over any row-level match. Row-level recovery — the root\\n   matches and every row is `identical?` to a registered packet's\\n   row in order — is taken only when exactly one distinct origin\\n   function matches: distinct occurrences of identical content share\\n   row objects (`packet-of` retrieves them from one index), so a\\n   first-match pick among ambiguous row-level candidates would\\n   attach another occurrence's origin. Ambiguity is refused (nil),\\n   which downstream reads as missing provenance, never as a guess.\\\"\\n  [lineage c]\\n  (when (and lineage (packet? c) (nil? (origin-of-fn c)))\\n    (let [entries (filter #(= (:root %) (first c)) @lineage)\\n          rows (second c)\\n          only-fn (fn [es]\\n                    (let [fns (distinct (map :fn es))]\\n                      (when (= 1 (count fns)) (first fns))))\\n          exact (filter #(identical? (:rows %) rows) entries)]\\n      (if (seq exact)\\n        (only-fn exact)\\n        (only-fn (filter #(and (= (count (:rows %)) (count rows))\\n                               (every? true?\\n                                       (map identical? (:rows %) rows)))\\n                         entries))))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private missing-provenance-origin\\n  \\\"The origin an unaccounted child answers below its coordinate: no\\n   sanctioned operation threaded provenance to it, so nothing may\\n   relabel its occurrences as this expansion's construction (P1-1).\\n   `output-correspondence` carries the marker through and\\n   `expand-call` refuses the output as `:missing-provenance`; the\\n   marker never reaches a table, a row, or an event.\\\"\\n  [:expansion ::missing])\\n\\n\\n(defn- constructed-origin-fn\\n  \\\"The origin function of a packet the prelude constructed: its root\\n   occurrence carries the pending expansion origin; a relative path\\n   below a child coordinate delegates to the packet passed for that\\n   child — a spliced operand retains its own incoming origin, however\\n   the macro's code embedded it. `recover` answers a child packet's\\n   retained origin function when the packet itself carries none (it\\n   was rebuilt by decomposition/reassembly); a child neither source\\n   accounts for answers `missing-provenance-origin` below its\\n   coordinate, which the expander refuses rather than reads as\\n   construction.\\\"\\n  ([coord-children] (constructed-origin-fn coord-children (fn [_] nil)))\\n  ([coord-children recover]\\n   (fn [rel]\\n     (if (empty? rel)\\n       pending-expansion-origin\\n       (if-some [child (get coord-children (first rel))]\\n         (if-some [f (or (origin-of-fn child) (recover child))]\\n           (f (subvec rel 1))\\n           missing-provenance-origin)\\n         pending-expansion-origin)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- construct\\n  \\\"Intern `body` over the rows of its child `packets`; a closed packet.\\n   Every node interned here is CONSTRUCTED by the expansion's code\\n   (ruling U1), so the result packet carries the construction origin\\n   function and the output's per-occurrence origins are read from this\\n   construction history — never inferred by comparing content\\n   addresses: a constructed node whose content equals a retained\\n   node's stays distinct, because its origin answers through this\\n   function and not through its address.\\n\\n   Values entering `:data`/`:key` slots are stripped of private\\n   tracking metadata first (P1-2), before hashing and validation.\\n   Missing child provenance never proves construction (P1-1): the\\n   result's origin function answers the missing marker below an\\n   unaccounted child and the expander refuses such an output.\\\"\\n  ([body packets] (construct body packets nil))\\n  ([body packets lineage]\\n   (let [recover (fn [child] (lineage-origin-for lineage child))]\\n     (let [tag (first body)\\n           slots (get working-grammar tag)\\n           body (if (and slots (= (count slots) (count (rest body))))\\n                  (into [tag]\\n                        (map (fn [[_ kind] v]\\n                               (if (contains? #{:data :key} kind)\\n                                 (strip-tracking v)\\n                                 v))\\n                             slots\\n                             (rest body)))\\n                  body)\\n           index (merge-packets packets)\\n           [index a] (intern-body index body)\\n           coord-children (into {}\\n                                (map (fn [[place p]] [(first place) p]))\\n                                (map vector (child-places (get index a)) packets))]\\n       (with-origin-of (packet-of index a)\\n         (constructed-origin-fn coord-children recover))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- root-row\\n  [[root rows]]\\n  (some #(when (= root (first %)) %) rows))\\n\\n\\n(defn- literal-packet\\n  ([v] (literal-packet v nil))\\n  ([v lineage] (construct [:literal (strip-tracking v)] [] lineage)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- application-packet\\n  ([operator operands] (application-packet operator operands nil))\\n  ([operator operands lineage]\\n   (construct [:application (first operator) (mapv first operands) false]\\n              (cons operator operands)\\n              lineage)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- lambda-packet\\n  ([params body] (lambda-packet params body nil))\\n  ([params body lineage]\\n   (when-not (and (sequential? params) (every? symbol? params))\\n     (throw-prelude \\\"yin/lambda params must be symbols\\\" {:op 'yin/lambda}))\\n   (doseq [p params] (check-guest-name! 'yin/lambda p))\\n   (construct [:lambda (vec params) (first body)] [body] lineage)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- name-of\\n  [tree]\\n  (let [row (root-row (need-packet 'yin/name-of tree))\\n        v (nth row 2 nil)]\\n    (cond (= :variable (row-tag row)) v\\n          (and (= :literal (row-tag row)) (symbol? v)) v\\n          (and (= :literal (row-tag row)) (string? v)) (symbol v)\\n          :else (throw-prelude \\\"yin/name-of reads a variable, literal symbol, or literal string\\\"\\n                               {:op 'yin/name-of}))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- sequence-body\\n  \\\"The reference `do` lowering: `(do a b c)` is `((fn [_] ((fn [_] c) b)) a)`;\\n   one form is itself and none is the literal nil.\\\"\\n  ([trees] (sequence-body trees nil))\\n  ([trees lineage]\\n   (let [trees (vec trees)]\\n     (doseq [t trees] (need-packet 'yin/sequence-body t))\\n     (case (count trees)\\n       0 (literal-packet nil lineage)\\n       1 (first trees)\\n       (application-packet\\n         (lambda-packet ['_]\\n                        (sequence-body (subvec trees 1) lineage)\\n                        lineage)\\n         [(first trees)]\\n         lineage)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- slot-of\\n  ([tree n] (slot-of tree n nil))\\n  ([tree n lineage]\\n   (let [row (root-row (need-packet 'yin/slot tree))\\n        kinds (into {} (slot-kinds (row-tag row)))\\n        index (packet-index tree)\\n        ;; an extracted slot is input syntax READ, not built: the\\n        ;; extracted packet retains the source packet's construction\\n        ;; origin function, descending through the slot's coordinate.\\n        ;; A source no operation accounted for answers the missing\\n        ;; marker, never fabricated construction (P1-1).\\n        ofn (or (origin-of-fn tree)\\n                (lineage-origin-for lineage tree)\\n                (fn [_] missing-provenance-origin))]\\n    (when-not (and (integer? n) (contains? kinds n))\\n      (throw-prelude \\\"yin/slot position names no grammar slot\\\"\\n                     {:op 'yin/slot, :position n}))\\n    (case (get kinds n)\\n      :node (with-origin-of (packet-of index (nth row n))\\n              (fn [rel] (ofn (into [n] rel))))\\n      :nodes (map-indexed (fn [i c]\\n                            (with-origin-of (packet-of index c)\\n                              (fn [rel] (ofn (into [[n i]] rel)))))\\n                          (nth row n))\\n      (nth row n)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn prelude\\n  \\\"The closed body environment (§5.3): the pure standard primitives (no\\n   `require`, no stream or FFI constructor) and the row-native constructors,\\n   each consuming and returning self-contained tree packets. `gensym` is the\\n   invocation-local `yin/gensym-sym`.\\n\\n   The collection primitives are provenance-aware within one invocation\\n   (P1-1): decomposing a packet (`first`/`rest`/`get`) registers its\\n   lineage, and reassembling one (`conj`/`assoc`/`vec`) reattaches the\\n   retained origin function when the rebuilt packet holds the very row\\n   objects it was decomposed from. A packet rebuilt any other way\\n   carries no provenance and is refused downstream rather than\\n   relabelled as construction.\\n\\n   Tracking metadata is valid only for the exact vector object it was\\n   attached to (P1-4): host `assoc`/`conj` copy the input vector's\\n   metadata onto a new vector whose contents may be unrelated, so\\n   every content-changing primitive routes its result through the one\\n   choke point `recover-rebuilt-packet`, which drops `::origin-of`\\n   before lineage recovery. Extractive primitives (`first`/`get`)\\n   return the very object that was tagged, so they alone may trust\\n   the metadata they hand back. A rebuilt packet no lineage entry\\n   accounts for carries no provenance and is refused downstream.\\\"\\n  ([gensym] (prelude gensym (atom [])))\\n  ([gensym lineage]\\n   (let [recover-packet (fn [result]\\n                          (cond (not (packet? result)) result\\n                                (origin-of-fn result)\\n                                (register-lineage! lineage result)\\n                                :else (if-some [f (lineage-origin-for\\n                                                    lineage result)]\\n                                        (with-origin-of result f)\\n                                        result)))\\n         recover-rebuilt-packet\\n         (fn [result]\\n           (recover-packet (if (origin-of-fn result)\\n                             (vary-meta result dissoc ::origin-of)\\n                             result)))\\n         wrap (fn [sym f]\\n                (let [entry (get vm/primitives sym)]\\n                  (if (and (map? entry)\\n                           (contains? entry :yin.k/function))\\n                    (assoc entry :yin.k/function f)\\n                    f)))\\n         p-first (fn [coll]\\n                   (register-lineage! lineage coll)\\n                   (recover-packet (first coll)))\\n         p-rest (fn [coll]\\n                  (register-lineage! lineage coll)\\n                  (vec (rest coll)))\\n         p-conj (fn [coll & xs]\\n                  (register-lineage! lineage coll)\\n                  (doseq [x xs] (register-lineage! lineage x))\\n                  (recover-rebuilt-packet (apply conj coll xs)))\\n         p-assoc (fn [m & kvs]\\n                   (register-lineage! lineage m)\\n                   (recover-rebuilt-packet (apply assoc m kvs)))\\n         p-get (fn\\n                 ([m k]\\n                  (register-lineage! lineage m)\\n                  (recover-packet (get m k)))\\n                 ([m k not-found]\\n                  (register-lineage! lineage m)\\n                  (recover-packet (get m k not-found))))\\n         p-vec (fn [coll]\\n                 (register-lineage! lineage coll)\\n                 (recover-rebuilt-packet (vec coll)))]\\n     (merge\\n       (dissoc vm/primitives 'require 'try-require 'bytes->str)\\n       {'first (wrap 'first p-first),\\n        'rest (wrap 'rest p-rest),\\n        'conj (wrap 'conj p-conj),\\n        'assoc (wrap 'assoc p-assoc),\\n        'get (wrap 'get p-get),\\n        'vec (wrap 'vec p-vec),\\n        'yin/tag (fn [tree] (row-tag (root-row (need-packet 'yin/tag tree)))),\\n        'yin/slot (fn [tree n] (slot-of tree n lineage)),\\n        'yin/literal (fn [v] (literal-packet v lineage)),\\n        'yin/variable (fn [sym]\\n                        (when-not (symbol? sym)\\n                          (throw-prelude \\\"yin/variable expects a symbol\\\" {:op 'yin/variable}))\\n                        (check-guest-name! 'yin/variable sym)\\n                        (construct [:variable sym] [] lineage)),\\n        'yin/lambda (fn [params body]\\n                      (lambda-packet params (need-packet 'yin/lambda body)\\n                                     lineage)),\\n        'yin/application (fn [operator operands]\\n                           (need-packet 'yin/application operator)\\n                           (doseq [o operands] (need-packet 'yin/application o))\\n                           (application-packet operator (vec operands)\\n                                               lineage)),\\n        'yin/if (fn [t c a]\\n                  (doseq [x [t c a]] (need-packet 'yin/if x))\\n                  (construct [:if (first t) (first c) (first a)] [t c a]\\n                             lineage)),\\n        'yin/sequence-body (fn [trees] (sequence-body trees lineage)),\\n        'yin/make-lambda (fn [params-tree body]\\n                           (let [row (root-row (need-packet 'yin/make-lambda params-tree))]\\n                             (when-not (and (= :literal (row-tag row))\\n                                            (vector? (nth row 2))\\n                                            (every? symbol? (nth row 2)))\\n                               (throw-prelude \\\"yin/make-lambda reads a literal parameter vector\\\"\\n                                              {:op 'yin/make-lambda}))\\n                             (lambda-packet (nth row 2)\\n                                            (need-packet 'yin/make-lambda body)\\n                                            lineage))),\\n        'yin/make-def (fn [name-tree value-tree]\\n                        (let [n (name-of name-tree)]\\n                          (check-guest-name! 'yin/make-def n)\\n                          (application-packet\\n                            (construct [:variable 'yin/def] [] lineage)\\n                            [(literal-packet n lineage)\\n                             (need-packet 'yin/make-def value-tree)]\\n                            lineage))),\\n        'yin/name-of name-of,\\n        'yin/gensym-sym gensym}))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private suspending-tags\\n  #{:vm/park :vm/resume :dao.stream.apply/call})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private effect-tags\\n  #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close})\\n\\n\\n(defn- catch-message\\n  [e]\\n  (or (ex-message e) (str e)))\\n\\n\\n(defn bounded-row-evaluator\\n  \\\"The default body runner (§5.2): a fresh throwaway ast-walker loaded from\\n   the lambda body's rows, its parameters bound in `env`, the closed\\n   `prelude` as its only primitives, no store, no module resolver, no stream\\n   constructor, and no FFI bridge. It counts VM transitions.\\n\\n   Returns `{:value v}`, or `{:error e}` with `e` one of\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '285,365p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '2440,2695p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '2730,2860p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; rg -n 'dsg__|reserved-prefix|macroexpand|m/invoke|macro/invoke' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl* test/yin/vm/macro* test/yin/repl*; sed -n '1,170p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py; type -a python3 clj-kondo clj\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '285,365p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '2440,2695p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; sed -n '2730,2860p' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc; rg -n 'dsg__|reserved-prefix|macroexpand|m/invoke|macro/invoke' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yin/repl* test/yin/vm/macro* test/yin/repl*; sed -n '1,170p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py; type -a python3 clj-kondo clj\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\";; =============================================================================\\n;; Private engine resources and sealed references (yin.vm.linker.md section\\n;; 7.3, r8 to r11)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;;\\n;; Stream handles, cursor cells, the FFI pair, and the link pair live in the\\n;; private `:resources` table of VM state. The engine's own machinery reads\\n;; and writes only that table; no store instruction and no `resolve-var`\\n;; step consults it. A program holds only references, and every reference\\n;; the engine issues carries a seal over its kind and id under the task's\\n;; capability secret, which the composition minted and which lives in VM\\n;; state alone. Every effect that resolves a program-supplied reference\\n;; verifies the seal first: a literal with a wrong or missing seal fails\\n;; closed with `:forged-resource-reference`.\\n\\n(defn- seal-of\\n  \\\"The seal of a `kind` reference to resource `id` under `secret`.\\\"\\n  [secret kind id]\\n  (jing/content-hash [:yin.k/seal secret kind id] {:algorithm :blake3}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- cursor-cell?\\n  \\\"True when a resource is a cursor cell, `{:stream-id id :cursor c}`.\\n   A cursor cell is the VM's mutable box (with identity) holding one\\n   immutable `dao.stream` cursor; `:stream/next` overwrites its `:cursor`\\n   with the successor. A guest program holds a sealed `:cursor-ref` naming\\n   it, never the cell itself. Not to be confused with `yin.vm.cell`, the\\n   general heap box. Vocabulary: UCF §7.5.3, \\\\\\\"Terminology\\\\\\\".\\\"\\n  [resource]\\n  (and (map? resource) (contains? resource :stream-id)))\\n\\n\\n(defn issue-ref\\n  \\\"A reference of `kind` (`:stream-ref`, `:cursor-ref`, or `:cell-ref`) to\\n   resource or heap cell `id`, sealed under the task's capability secret.\\n   A task composed\\n   without a secret cannot issue one: it fails closed rather than issue a\\n   reference anyone could forge.\\\"\\n  [state kind id]\\n  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)]\\n    (when (nil? secret)\\n      (fail (str \\\"This task has no capability secret, so it cannot issue\\\"\\n                 \\\" a resource reference\\\")\\n            {:reason :no-capability-secret, :kind kind, :id id}))\\n    {:type kind, :id id, :seal (seal-of secret kind id)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn authentic-ref?\\n  \\\"True when `ref` is a `kind` reference this task issued: its seal\\n   verifies under the task's secret, and it names a live resource of that\\n   kind. A `:cell-ref` is checked against `:heap` instead: its id is live\\n   by `contains?` (a cell may hold nil) and its seal equals the one the\\n   heap entry recorded at allocation, so no hash is computed per access.\\\"\\n  [state kind ref]\\n  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)\\n        id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))]\\n    (if (= :cell-ref kind)\\n      (let [heap (:heap state)]\\n        (boolean (and (some? secret)\\n                      (= kind (:type ref))\\n                      (contains? heap id)\\n                      (= (:seal ref) (:seal (get heap id))))))\\n      (let [resource (get (:resources state) id)]\\n        (boolean (and (some? secret)\\n                      (= kind (:type ref))\\n                      (some? resource)\\n                      (= (:seal ref) (seal-of secret kind id))\\n                      (= (= :cursor-ref kind) (cursor-cell? resource))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn check-ref!\\n  \\\"The resource id a program-supplied `kind` reference names, once its\\n   seal verifies (r10). Anything else fails closed with\\n   `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it named;\\n   a cell ref whose id the heap does not hold is\\n   `:dead-or-forged-reference` instead: its cell was swept, or never was.\\n   Ids are never reused, so such a ref is never silently reallocated.\\\"\\n  [state effect-kind kind ref]\\n  (if (authentic-ref? state kind ref)\\n    (:id ref)\\n    (let [id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))\\n             :yin.k/effects declared})\\n      state)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- heap-write\\n  \\\"`state` with heap cell `id` holding `v` under `seal`: allocation when the\\n   cell is new, a write (the seal unchanged) when it is live. A write\\n   preserves the entry's `:provenance` sibling; only the mint attaches one,\\n   and only at allocation.\\\"\\n  [state id seal v]\\n  (update-in state [:heap id] (fn [entry] (assoc entry :value v, :seal seal))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Heap reclamation (Architect heap-reclamation design, slice 1)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;;\\n;; A deterministic, allocation-triggered, stop-the-world mark-sweep over\\n;; `:heap`, as a pure `vm -> vm'`. The `:cell/new` arm counts allocations in\\n;; `:gc` and collects when the count reaches the threshold, so two runs\\n;; collect at the same allocation. Marking runs over the heap as it was when\\n;; the cycle began (a persistent map, so the snapshot is a reference) and the\\n;; sweep removes only ids of that snapshot: an id allocated at or after the\\n;; cycle's counter start is not in it and is spared. A budgeted marker can\\n;; later run the same `trace` k steps per round. Ids are never reused:\\n;; `:id-counter` only grows, so a ref to a swept cell names nothing and is\\n;; refused `:dead-or-forged-reference`.\\n\\n(defn- heap-ref-id\\n  \\\"The id the map `x` names when it is a `:cell-ref` authentic against\\n   `heap`, else nil.\\\"\\n  [heap x]\\n  (when (= :cell-ref (:type x))\\n    (let [id (:id x)\\n          entry (get heap id)]\\n      (when (and (some? entry) (= (:seal x) (:seal entry))) id))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- moded\\n  \\\"`work` with each of `xs` pushed in `mode`, `:kernel` or `:data`.\\\"\\n  [work mode xs]\\n  (into work (map (fn [x] [mode x])) xs))\\n\\n\\n(defn- identity-hash\\n  \\\"The host's identity hash of `o`: one number per object, whatever the\\n   object's size. It reads no field of `o`, so a continuation holding a\\n   thousand-frame chain costs what a scalar costs.\\\"\\n  [o]\\n  #?(:clj (System/identityHashCode o)\\n     :cljs (goog/getUid o)\\n     :cljd (dart-core/identityHashCode o)))\\n\\n\\n;; `trace` cannot key its seen set on `(hash [mode value])`, the shape a\\n;; Clojure set asks for: a closure's and a continuation's `-hash` is\\n;; structural over kind, owner and payload by law (`yin.vm.values`, table C),\\n;; so hashing one recurses through its whole payload — the captured\\n;; environments, the continuation chain below, the AST bodies those hold. A\\n;; guest that has grown a 1000-frame handler chain then exhausts the host\\n;; stack inside the collector, long before its own recursion guard fires\\n;; (stack audit r2: a 13,542-frame trace, every frame a `hash`).\\n;;\\n;; Identity is also the sounder key, and not only the cheaper one. Nothing\\n;; is missed by it: a value already walked is the same object, and an\\n;; equal-but-distinct one is walked again, finding the same ids. `found` —\\n;; hence the heap the sweep leaves — is what value-keyed dedup computed.\\n(deftype Visited\\n  [mode o]\\n  #?@(:cljd [cljd.core/IEquiv\\n             (-equiv [_ other]\\n                     (and (instance? Visited other)\\n                          (= mode (.-mode ^Visited other))\\n                          (identical? o (.-o ^Visited other))))\\n             cljd.core/IHash\\n             (-hash [_] (bit-xor (hash mode) (identity-hash o)))]\\n      :clj [Object\\n            (equals [_ other]\\n                    (and (instance? Visited other)\\n                         (= mode (.-mode ^Visited other))\\n                         (identical? o (.-o ^Visited other))))\\n            (hashCode [_] (bit-xor (hash mode) (identity-hash o)))\\n            clojure.lang.IHashEq\\n            (hasheq [_] (bit-xor (hash mode) (identity-hash o)))]\\n      :cljs [IEquiv\\n             (-equiv [_ other]\\n                     (and (instance? Visited other)\\n                          (= mode (.-mode ^Visited other))\\n                          (identical? o (.-o ^Visited other))))\\n             IHash\\n             (-hash [_] (bit-xor (hash mode) (identity-hash o)))]))\\n\\n\\n(defn- trace\\n  \\\"`found` plus the ids of `heap` reachable from `roots`, a map\\n   `{:kernel [...] :data [...]}` of root values by mode. Two modes keep\\n   guest data from being read as kernel shapes (gemini reclamation gate,\\n   concern 1):\\n\\n   - `:kernel`, a value in a kernel position (a kernel root, an engine\\n     table entry, a closure or continuation payload, or a kernel child of\\n     one): the kernel's `gc-children` may interpret it, and answers which\\n     of its children are kernel positions and which are values. A value\\n     it does not recognize is walked whole, as data.\\n   - `:data`, a value position (an environment value, an operand, a\\n     register, a cell's content): every map is walked whole, never\\n     pruned, so a guest map shaped like a frame cannot hide a cell ref.\\n     Only a closure or continuation, a host type no guest can forge,\\n     re-enters `:kernel` mode through its payload.\\n\\n   Scalars hold nothing. With `follow?` a newly found id's content is\\n   traced too, as data (marking); without, only the refs met directly\\n   (pinning). Each object is visited once per mode, so shared environments\\n   cost one walk and a cell holding its own ref terminates; the visit is\\n   keyed on identity (`Visited`), never on a value's structural hash,\\n   which for a closure or a continuation would recurse through the whole\\n   chain it holds. The worklist is explicit: a deep continuation chain\\n   does not grow the host stack.\\\"\\n  [vm heap roots found follow?]\\n  (let [children-of (if (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel vm)\\n                      #(module/gc-children vm %)\\n                      (constantly nil))]\\n    (loop [work (-> ()\\n                    (moded :kernel (:kernel roots))\\n                    (moded :data (:data roots)))\\n           seen #{}\\n           found found]\\n      (if (empty? work)\\n        found\\n        (let [[mode x] (peek work)\\n              work (pop work)\\n              visit (when-not (scalar? x) (Visited. mode x))]\\n          (if (or (nil? visit) (contains? seen visit))\\n            (recur work seen found)\\n            (let [id (when (map? x) (heap-ref-id heap x))\\n                  new-id? (and (some? id) (not (contains? found id)))\\n                  children (when (and (= :kernel mode)\\n                                      (not (values/host-typed? x)))\\n                             (children-of x))\\n                  work (cond-> work\\n                         (and new-id? follow?)\\n                         (-> (conj [:data (:value (get heap id))])\\n                             (conj [:data (:provenance (get heap id))]))\\n                         (values/host-typed? x)\\n                         (conj [:kernel (values/payload x)])\\n                         (some? children)\\n                         (-> (moded :kernel (:kernel children))\\n                             (moded :data (:values children)))\\n                         (and (nil? children) (map? x))\\n                         (-> (moded :data (keys x))\\n                             (moded :data (vals x)))\\n                         (and (nil? children) (not (map? x)) (coll? x))\\n                         (moded :data x))]\\n              (recur work\\n                     (conj seen visit)\\n                     (if new-id? (conj found id) found)))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- gc-roots\\n  \\\"Every root of `vm` (design Q1), by mode: the kernel's registers as it\\n   classifies them (`module/gc-roots`), each entry of `:parked`,\\n   `:wait-set` and `:ready-queue` as a kernel position, the store and\\n   every module store as values, then `extra` as values. Code, images,\\n   the registry, primitives, `:callable-effects`, telemetry and\\n   `:resources` are not roots: a literal can never be an authentic ref,\\n   and a value inside a stream is the medium's (hence pinning).\\\"\\n  [vm extra]\\n  (let [kernel (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel vm)\\n                 (module/gc-roots vm))]\\n    {:kernel (-> (vec (:kernel kernel))\\n                 (into (vals (:parked vm)))\\n                 (into (:wait-set vm))\\n                 (into (:ready-queue vm))),\\n     :data (-> [(:store vm) (:module-stores vm)]\\n               (into (:values kernel))\\n               (into extra))}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- mark\\n  \\\"The ids of `heap` live from `roots` and from the `pinned` ids: a pinned\\n   cell is a root, so its content — value and provenance — is traced too,\\n   as data.\\\"\\n  [vm heap roots pinned]\\n  (let [pins (filterv #(contains? heap %) pinned)]\\n    (trace vm\\n           heap\\n           (update roots :data\\n                   into (mapcat (fn [id] (let [e (get heap id)]\\n                                           [(:value e) (:provenance e)]))\\n                                pins))\\n           (set pins)\\n           true)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- sweep\\n  \\\"`heap` without each id of the cycle's `snapshot` that `marked` lacks.\\n   An id allocated after the snapshot is not in it, so it is spared.\\\"\\n  [heap snapshot marked]\\n  (reduce-kv (fn [h id _] (if (contains? marked id) h (dissoc h id)))\\n             heap\\n             snapshot))\\n\\n\\n(defn- gc-of\\n  [state]\\n  (or (:gc state) (vm/fresh-gc nil)))\\n\\n\\n(defn collect\\n  \\\"`vm` after one stop-the-world collection of its `:heap`, with `extra`\\n   traced as additional roots. The allocation count restarts and the\\n   threshold becomes `max(base, 2 x live)`, so the total work stays linear\\n   in allocations. Pure: the same state always collects to the same heap.\\\"\\n  ([vm] (collect vm nil))\\n  ([vm extra]\\n   (let [gc (gc-of vm)\\n         snapshot (:heap vm)\\n         marked (mark vm snapshot (gc-roots vm extra) (:pinned gc))\\n         heap (sweep (:heap vm) snapshot marked)]\\n     (assoc vm\\n            :heap heap\\n            :gc (assoc gc\\n                       :since 0\\n                       :threshold (max (:base gc) (* 2 (count heap))))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- count-allocation\\n  \\\"`state` with one allocation counted, collected first when the count\\n   reaches the threshold. `roots`, the values being boxed (the content, and\\n   for a mint the provenance), are held by no register yet, so each is\\n   traced as an extra root.\\\"\\n  [state & roots]\\n  (let [gc (gc-of state)\\n        since (inc (:since gc))]\\n    (if (>= since (:threshold gc))\\n      (collect state (vec roots))\\n      (assoc state :gc (assoc gc :since since)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn pin-refs\\n  \\\"`state` with every cell `v` holds a ref to pinned for the task's life\\n   (owner decision 1). A value appended to a stream or carried by an FFI\\n   request leaves the VM's view, so no trace could find a ref inside it\\n   when it comes back.\\\"\\n  [state v]\\n  (if (or (scalar? v) (empty? (:heap state)))\\n    state\\n    (let [ids (trace state (:heap state) {:data [v]} #{} false)]\\n      (if (seq ids)\\n        (assoc state :gc (update (gc-of state) :pinned into ids))\\n        state))))\\n\\n\\n(defn handle-effect\\n  \\\"Dispatch an effect and return {:state updated-state :value v :blocked? bool}.\\n   park-entry-fns maps :stream/put and :stream/next to functions that build\\n            {:state new-state, :value cursor-ref, :blocked? false})\\n          :stream/put\\n          (let [state (pin-refs state (:val effect))\\n                result (handle-put state effect)]\\n            (if (:park result)\\n              (let [built-entry (when park-entry\\n                                  (park-entry state effect result))\\n                    built-entry (if (and built-entry (not (:datom built-entry)))\\n                                  (assoc built-entry :datom (:val effect))\\n                                  built-entry)]\\n                (if (and built-entry (= :running (vm/gate-mode state)))\\n                  (let [[n s'] (issue-number (:state result))]\\n                    (handle-stream-block\\n                      (assoc result :state s')\\n                      (assoc built-entry :yin.k/issue n)))\\n                  (handle-stream-block result built-entry)))\\n              {:state (:state result),\\n               :value (:value result),\\n               :blocked? false}))\\n          :stream/next\\n          (let [result (handle-next state effect)]\\n            (if (:park result)\\n              (let [built-entry (when park-entry\\n                                  (park-entry state effect result))]\\n                (handle-stream-block result built-entry))\\n              {:state (:state result),\\n               :value (:value result),\\n               :blocked? false}))\\n          :stream/poll\\n          (let [result (handle-poll state effect)]\\n            (if (and (:park result) (= :running (vm/gate-mode state)))\\n              (let [builder (or (get park-entry-fns :stream/poll)\\n                                (get park-entry-fns :stream/next))\\n                    built (when builder (builder state effect result))]\\n                (handle-stream-block\\n                  result\\n                  (when built\\n                    (assoc built\\n                           :reason :observe\\n                           :op :poll\\n                           :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result)\\n                           :stream-id (:stream-id result)))))\\n              {:state (:state result),\\n               :value (:value result),\\n               :blocked? false}))\\n          :stream/close\\n          (let [_ (gate-refuse! state effect)\\n                close-result (handle-close state effect)]\\n            {:state (:state close-result), :value nil, :blocked? false})\\n          ;; `:heap` is VM state, not continuation state: a write is seen\\n          ;; by every holder of the ref and survives continuation invocation\\n          :cell/new\\n          (let [[id s'] (gensym (count-allocation state (:val effect)) \\\"cell\\\")\\n                ref (issue-ref s' :cell-ref id)]\\n            {:state (heap-write s' id (:seal ref) (:val effect)),\\n             :value ref,\\n             :blocked? false})\\n          :cell/get\\n          (let [id (check-ref! state :cell/get :cell-ref (:cell effect))]\\n            ;; the content is answered as data, never re-interpreted\\n            {:state state,\\n             :value (get-in state [:heap id :value]),\\n             :blocked? false})\\n          :cell/set!\\n          (let [id (check-ref! state :cell/set! :cell-ref (:cell effect))]\\n            {:state (heap-write state id (get-in state [:heap id :seal])\\n                                (:val effect)),\\n             :value (:val effect),\\n             :blocked? false})\\n          ;; The cell-ref ruling of record: the same heap+seal predicate\\n          ;; `check-ref!` consults (r10), answered as data instead of\\n          ;; failing closed, so compiled call routing can ask the issuer\\n          ;; whether it issued a reference. A pure query: no state change,\\n          ;; never fatal, and the engine learns no frontend semantics.\\n          :cell/authentic?\\n          {:state state,\\n           :value (authentic-ref? state :cell-ref (:cell effect)),\\n           :blocked? false}\\n          ;; The privileged mint: a fresh sealed cell with `:value` and a\\n          ;; private `:provenance` sibling installed atomically. Provenance\\n          ;; is opaque to the engine; it is answered only by `:cell/provenance`.\\n          :cell/new-provenance\\n          (let [[id s'] (gensym (count-allocation state\\n                                                  (:val effect)\\n                                                  (:provenance effect))\\n                                \\\"cell\\\")\\n                ref (issue-ref s' :cell-ref id)]\\n            {:state (assoc-in s' [:heap id] {:value (:val effect),\\n                                             :seal (:seal ref),\\n                                             :provenance (:provenance effect)}),\\n             :value ref,\\n             :blocked? false})\\n          ;; The private reader: authenticate before consulting the slot, so\\n          ;; an unauthenticated candidate answers nil instead of refusing.\\n          ;; Never fatal, never mutating.\\n          :cell/provenance\\n          (let [ref (:cell effect)]\\n            {:state state,\\n             :value (when (authentic-ref? state :cell-ref ref)\\n                      (get-in state [:heap (:id ref) :provenance])),\\n             :blocked? false})\\n          (let [[id s'] (gensym state \\\"effect\\\")\\n                handler (module/get-effect-handler (:modules state)\\n                                                   (:effect effect))]\\n            (if handler\\n              (handler s' effect (assoc opts :id id))\\n              (fail \\\"Unknown effect\\\" {:effect (:effect effect)}))))]\\n    (update result\\n            :state\\n            (fn [result-state]\\n              (telemetry/emit-snapshot (assoc result-state\\n                                              :value (:value result))\\n                                       :effect\\n                                       {:effect-type (:effect effect)})))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Program Cache Machinery\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def default-compiled-cache-limit 8)\\n\\n\\n(defn build-program-index\\n  [datoms]\\n  (group-by first (vec datoms)))\\n\\n\\n(defn frame-versions\\n  [frame]\\n  (loop [f frame\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:724:    (is (= :variable (m/invoke (lam '(fn [t] (yin/tag t))) [v] cx))\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:727:                    (m/invoke (lam '(fn [t] (yin/literal (yin/name-of t)))) [v] cx)))\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:729:                    (m/invoke (lam '(fn [app] (yin/slot app 2)))\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:732:    (is (= 'x (m/invoke (lam '(fn [t] (yin/slot t 2))) [v] cx)))\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:733:    (is (= :arity (:kind (throws-data #(m/invoke (lam '(fn [a] a)) [] cx)))))\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:734:    (is (= :body-error (:kind (throws-data #(m/invoke (lam '(fn [t] (yin/slot t 1))) [v] cx))))\\ntest/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc:737:                    (m/invoke (lam '(fn [p b] (yin/make-lambda p b)))\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:388:;; Generated names carry the reserved `dsg__` prefix and derive from the\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:410:(def ^:private reserved-prefix \\\"dsg__\\\")\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:420:  (= reserved-prefix (subs (name sym) 0 (min (count (name sym))\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:421:           